window.WLE_DOC_INDEX=[{"title":"Home","cat":"start","url":"index.html","text":"Launchstation Documentation A dashboard-first PlayStation 2 file manager and launcher for real hardware: file management, applications, APA/PFS, MMCE, PC transfers, backups, POPStarter VCDs, Ember PS1 images and Neutrino PS2 ISOs. FILE MANAGER NEUTRINO ISO POPSTARTER VCD EMBER PS1 APA / PFS MMCE STORAGE PC TRANSFER Start here Download latest What this project is Launchstation keeps the power-user roots of LaunchELF/uLaunchELF/wLaunchELF while adding a PS2-style dashboard, safer device handling, modern storage paths, searchable app discovery, richer file operations, MMCE workflows, PC interoperability and external game-core handoffs. The classic browser remains available; the dashboard is the primary interface. Recommended first steps Getting Started Install, first boot, first device, first app File Browser Copy, move, rename, delete, sort, timestamps PS2 ISO · Neutrino Launch .ISO images from supported devices PS1 VCD · POPStarter Launch POPStarter-compatible .VCD images PS1 Images · Ember Launch save-safe EMBER/games/<Game>/ images Wiki & Guides Feature map, troubleshooting, credits, offline docs Design goals Device-agnostic launching. Work from the device the user actually browsed instead of assuming mass0 or mc0. Demand-load expensive stacks. Drivers and networking stay out of startup until needed. Keep classic functionality. Dashboard actions wrap proven filesystem and browser operations instead of discarding them. Visible state. Not checked, absent, busy, unsaved changes and background work are explicit. Hardware-first validation. CI catches structural/link/layout regressions; device behavior is still verified on consoles. Feature map Area Highlights Dashboard Home, Files, Tools, Settings; console model, region, ROM and DVD versions read at startup; Applications library nested in Home, Last folder shortcut; themes, clock, temperature, BGM and animation. File management Classic and dashboard browsers, copy/cut/paste, rename, delete, folders, PSU operations, VMC mounting, timestamp tools, sorting and metadata. Storage Memory cards, USB/MASS, MMCE, MX4SIO, internal APA/PFS, ATA/exFAT and supported network storage. Game images PS1 VCD via POPStarter; PS1 CUE/BIN/EXE folder layouts via Ember; PS2 ISO via Neutrino. Backups PSU save backups and metadata-preserving file operations. Built with TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK Our local and CI builds use the PS2BUILD SDK for EE/IOP toolchains, libraries and the ps2.yaml build workflow. See developer setup to contribute with the same SDK."},{"title":"Recommended first steps","cat":"start","url":"index.html#first-steps","text":"Getting Started Install, first boot, first device, first app File Browser Copy, move, rename, delete, sort, timestamps PS2 ISO · Neutrino Launch .ISO images from supported devices PS1 VCD · POPStarter Launch POPStarter-compatible .VCD images PS1 Images · Ember Launch save-safe EMBER/games/<Game>/ images Wiki & Guides Feature map, troubleshooting, credits, offline docs"},{"title":"Design goals","cat":"start","url":"index.html#design","text":"Device-agnostic launching. Work from the device the user actually browsed instead of assuming mass0 or mc0. Demand-load expensive stacks. Drivers and networking stay out of startup until needed. Keep classic functionality. Dashboard actions wrap proven filesystem and browser operations instead of discarding them. Visible state. Not checked, absent, busy, unsaved changes and background work are explicit. Hardware-first validation. CI catches structural/link/layout regressions; device behavior is still verified on consoles."},{"title":"Feature map","cat":"start","url":"index.html#feature-map","text":"Area Highlights Dashboard Home, Files, Tools, Settings; console model, region, ROM and DVD versions read at startup; Applications library nested in Home, Last folder shortcut; themes, clock, temperature, BGM and animation. File management Classic and dashboard browsers, copy/cut/paste, rename, delete, folders, PSU operations, VMC mounting, timestamp tools, sorting and metadata. Storage Memory cards, USB/MASS, MMCE, MX4SIO, internal APA/PFS, ATA/exFAT and supported network storage. Game images PS1 VCD via POPStarter; PS1 CUE/BIN/EXE folder layouts via Ember; PS2 ISO via Neutrino. Backups PSU save backups and metadata-preserving file operations. Built with TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK Our local and CI builds use the PS2BUILD SDK for EE/IOP toolchains, libraries and the ps2.yaml build workflow. See developer setup to contribute with the same SDK."},{"title":"Getting Started","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html","text":"Getting Started Install the correct app variant, open the four-tab dashboard and begin with the actual file browser. 1. Download the current package Use the verified rolling release . Choose APP_LAUNCHSTATION.zip for standard PS2 controls or APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.zip for the inherited PS3/PS4 controller variant. Matching .psu packages support memory-card installation via PSU Paste. Device-specific hardware testing is separate from successful compilation. 2. Install the app folder mc0:/ └── APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ ├── LAUNCHSTATION.ELF ├── title.cfg ├── BGM.ADP └── WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM Extract the ZIP as a folder or install the matching PSU. Preserve existing settings and customized sidecars if upgrading. The DS package has its own folder and LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF . BGM is optional to run; the current packages include it. 3. Use the four main tabs Use L1/R1 to move between Home, Files, Tools and Settings . Home includes the Applications library and, once known, Last folder. Files opens the native browser immediately ; choose a drive or folder there. Tools contains individual built-in commands and specialist utilities. Current UI map . 4. Open a device Start in Files, at the device root. Memory cards, USB/MASS, MMCE, MX4SIO, internal HDD/ATA and configured network devices have distinct availability rules. Not checked usually means opening the device must start a lazy-loaded driver. USB units can change numbering. Avoid disconnecting devices during file operations. 5. Find applications Use Home > Applications for the library, or browse and launch an ELF directly from Files. Under Settings > Home & applications > Application library , configure application folders and refresh the library. The default roots currently include mc:/APPS , mc:/BOOT , mass:/APPS and mass:/BOOT , one level deep. Applications outside these folders can still launch from Files. 6. Optional external cores Neutrino is needed for PS2 .ISO ; POPStarter for PS1 .VCD ; Ember for PS1 .CUE / .BIN / .EXE in its save-safe game-folder layout. These external programs and game/BIOS data are not bundled. Read the specific guide before your first handoff. 8. Save and update safely Settings has explicit Save/Discard semantics. Before upgrading, retain a backup of your configuration and current working ELF. The native updater verifies the rolling package and stages changes, with an original executable recovery copy for rollback."},{"title":"1. Download the current package","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#download","text":"Use the verified rolling release . Choose APP_LAUNCHSTATION.zip for standard PS2 controls or APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.zip for the inherited PS3/PS4 controller variant. Matching .psu packages support memory-card installation via PSU Paste. Device-specific hardware testing is separate from successful compilation."},{"title":"2. Install the app folder","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#install","text":"mc0:/ └── APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ ├── LAUNCHSTATION.ELF ├── title.cfg ├── BGM.ADP └── WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM Extract the ZIP as a folder or install the matching PSU. Preserve existing settings and customized sidecars if upgrading. The DS package has its own folder and LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF . BGM is optional to run; the current packages include it."},{"title":"3. Use the four main tabs","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#first-boot","text":"Use L1/R1 to move between Home, Files, Tools and Settings . Home includes the Applications library and, once known, Last folder. Files opens the native browser immediately ; choose a drive or folder there. Tools contains individual built-in commands and specialist utilities. Current UI map ."},{"title":"4. Open a device","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#device","text":"Start in Files, at the device root. Memory cards, USB/MASS, MMCE, MX4SIO, internal HDD/ATA and configured network devices have distinct availability rules. Not checked usually means opening the device must start a lazy-loaded driver. USB units can change numbering. Avoid disconnecting devices during file operations."},{"title":"5. Find applications","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#apps","text":"Use Home > Applications for the library, or browse and launch an ELF directly from Files. Under Settings > Home & applications > Application library , configure application folders and refresh the library. The default roots currently include mc:/APPS , mc:/BOOT , mass:/APPS and mass:/BOOT , one level deep. Applications outside these folders can still launch from Files."},{"title":"6. Optional external cores","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#external","text":"Neutrino is needed for PS2 .ISO ; POPStarter for PS1 .VCD ; Ember for PS1 .CUE / .BIN / .EXE in its save-safe game-folder layout. These external programs and game/BIOS data are not bundled. Read the specific guide before your first handoff."},{"title":"8. Save and update safely","cat":"start","url":"getting-started.html#update","text":"Settings has explicit Save/Discard semantics. Before upgrading, retain a backup of your configuration and current working ELF. The native updater verifies the rolling package and stages changes, with an original executable recovery copy for rollback."},{"title":"Install & Update","cat":"start","url":"install.html","text":"Install & Update Install the matching app package and preserve your existing configuration and customized files. Standard rolling package mc0:/ └── APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ ├── LAUNCHSTATION.ELF ├── title.cfg ├── BGM.ADP └── WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM Both standard ZIP and PSU packages contain those four files. DS34 controller packages use APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS with LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF. The ELF alone can run the dashboard, but the packaged updater CA supports native verified HTTPS updating; BGM.ADP is optional for a standalone installation. Choose your installer Download the matching ZIP/PSU from Downloads & Releases . Extract the ZIP app folder to readable storage or use the File Browser PSU Paste command to install a PSU on a memory card. Keep a separate copy of your previous working ELF, settings and customized sidecars. Configuration LAUNCHSTATION retains classic LAUNCHELF.CNF support and its dashboard settings store. The saved path can be configured next to the ELF, in a selected folder or on a memory-card SYS-CONF location. See Configuration . Native updates and rollback The current in-app updater checks release ancestry, downloads the matching standard or DS34 PSU, validates the published SHA-256 and ELF, stages replacement and keeps a .BAK copy of the preceding executable. It restores missing only CA/BGM sidecars from the verified four-file PSU without overwriting customized ones. Existing interrupted .UPDATE or .PACKAGE artifacts require manual resolution. Restart LAUNCHSTATION to use installed code; older clients may need a one-time manual package update. Preserve a hardware-tested baseline If a newer rolling build regresses on your console, retain the 2026-10-08 USB-tested baseline and collect hardware details before retrying. Compiled CI success is distinct from successful console validation."},{"title":"Standard rolling package","cat":"start","url":"install.html#package","text":"mc0:/ └── APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ ├── LAUNCHSTATION.ELF ├── title.cfg ├── BGM.ADP └── WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM Both standard ZIP and PSU packages contain those four files. DS34 controller packages use APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS with LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF. The ELF alone can run the dashboard, but the packaged updater CA supports native verified HTTPS updating; BGM.ADP is optional for a standalone installation."},{"title":"Choose your installer","cat":"start","url":"install.html#downloads","text":"Download the matching ZIP/PSU from Downloads & Releases . Extract the ZIP app folder to readable storage or use the File Browser PSU Paste command to install a PSU on a memory card. Keep a separate copy of your previous working ELF, settings and customized sidecars."},{"title":"Configuration","cat":"start","url":"install.html#config","text":"LAUNCHSTATION retains classic LAUNCHELF.CNF support and its dashboard settings store. The saved path can be configured next to the ELF, in a selected folder or on a memory-card SYS-CONF location. See Configuration ."},{"title":"Native updates and rollback","cat":"start","url":"install.html#native","text":"The current in-app updater checks release ancestry, downloads the matching standard or DS34 PSU, validates the published SHA-256 and ELF, stages replacement and keeps a .BAK copy of the preceding executable. It restores missing only CA/BGM sidecars from the verified four-file PSU without overwriting customized ones. Existing interrupted .UPDATE or .PACKAGE artifacts require manual resolution. Restart LAUNCHSTATION to use installed code; older clients may need a one-time manual package update."},{"title":"Preserve a hardware-tested baseline","cat":"start","url":"install.html#baseline","text":"If a newer rolling build regresses on your console, retain the 2026-10-08 USB-tested baseline and collect hardware details before retrying. Compiled CI success is distinct from successful console validation."},{"title":"Downloads & Releases","cat":"start","url":"releases.html","text":"Downloads & Releases Install the latest verified package and review the current Rolling changelog summary. Latest release Build checks Release assets Package Packed executable Installation APP_LAUNCHSTATION.zip LAUNCHSTATION.ELF Extract APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ to a supported filesystem. APP_LAUNCHSTATION.psu LAUNCHSTATION.ELF Use PSU Paste to install APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ on a memory card. APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.zip LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF Extract APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS/ to a supported filesystem. APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.psu LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF Use PSU Paste to install APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS/ on a memory card. Each package includes the ELF plus updater support files ( title.cfg , WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM , BGM.ADP ). ZIP and PSU payloads match. Upgrading an older installation: updaters expecting standalone ELFs or two-file PSUs need one manual installation of the new package before using the newer four-file PSU format. Back up existing settings before replacing an app folder. Update order could not be verified: this is a failed check, not the normal up-to-date result. Earlier PS2BUILD releases omitted their commit ID and show local in Build information. Those installs need one manual installation of a corrected package and a restart. A build matching the published commit should report Launchstation is up to date . Rolling changelog summary Infinite wrap-around navigation for dashboard lists and file-browser views. Safer replacement when pasting over existing files (old copy preserved until replacement is verified). Copy/delete overlays now show cleaner progress with filename, remaining size in MB and smooth progress fill. Cross-device copy reliability improvements for short reads/writes and storage handoff timing. Clock-save and storage-load reliability fixes across restart and card/driver transitions. Full details: latest release notes · CHANGELOG.md . Notes The -DS packages enable inherited PS3/PS4 controller support; standard packages keep the standard PS2 pad driver. CI success and package integrity do not replace real hardware validation. If a new rolling build regresses on your console, keep a known-good baseline and report console model, source hash and reproduction steps."},{"title":"Release assets","cat":"start","url":"releases.html#assets","text":"Package Packed executable Installation APP_LAUNCHSTATION.zip LAUNCHSTATION.ELF Extract APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ to a supported filesystem. APP_LAUNCHSTATION.psu LAUNCHSTATION.ELF Use PSU Paste to install APP_LAUNCHSTATION/ on a memory card. APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.zip LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF Extract APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS/ to a supported filesystem. APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS.psu LAUNCHSTATION-DS.ELF Use PSU Paste to install APP_LAUNCHSTATION-DS/ on a memory card. Each package includes the ELF plus updater support files ( title.cfg , WLE-UPDATE-CA.PEM , BGM.ADP ). ZIP and PSU payloads match. Upgrading an older installation: updaters expecting standalone ELFs or two-file PSUs need one manual installation of the new package before using the newer four-file PSU format. Back up existing settings before replacing an app folder. Update order could not be verified: this is a failed check, not the normal up-to-date result. Earlier PS2BUILD releases omitted their commit ID and show local in Build information. Those installs need one manual installation of a corrected package and a restart. A build matching the published commit should report Launchstation is up to date ."},{"title":"Rolling changelog summary","cat":"start","url":"releases.html#changelog","text":"Infinite wrap-around navigation for dashboard lists and file-browser views. Safer replacement when pasting over existing files (old copy preserved until replacement is verified). Copy/delete overlays now show cleaner progress with filename, remaining size in MB and smooth progress fill. Cross-device copy reliability improvements for short reads/writes and storage handoff timing. Clock-save and storage-load reliability fixes across restart and card/driver transitions. Full details: latest release notes · CHANGELOG.md ."},{"title":"Notes","cat":"start","url":"releases.html#notes","text":"The -DS packages enable inherited PS3/PS4 controller support; standard packages keep the standard PS2 pad driver. CI success and package integrity do not replace real hardware validation. If a new rolling build regresses on your console, keep a known-good baseline and report console model, source hash and reproduction steps."},{"title":"Dashboard","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html","text":"Dashboard: Current Navigation LAUNCHSTATION now has four visible top-level tabs. Applications live under Home; Files is the actual native File Browser, not an extra landing page. Four top-level tabs Tab Behavior in current builds Home Continue carousel, pinned/recent/favorite applications, Applications library shortcut, Last folder shortcut when available, and optional configured launch-key shortcuts. Files Opens the native File Browser directly inside the dashboard frame. Browse drives and folders, operate on files and return to the same folder/selection after switching tabs. Tools File Browser, Text Editor, USB unit diagnostics, HDD Manager, exploit installer, individual built-in and network tools. Settings Appearance, Home/app library, sound, input, launching, devices/MMCE, network, and system settings. Home and Applications Select Applications in Home > Browse & shortcuts to enter the full library without adding a fifth top-level tab. When Recent applications is set to 0 and no recent history is saved, entering Home opens this viewer automatically. Back returns to Browse & shortcuts. Selecting Applications as the Start screen opens the full library with All selected. The library keeps its Grid/List/Carousel views, favorites, pins, filtering and recents. SELECT cycles application views. Add or remove a favorite from the application options; the result also displays a toast. Last folder appears on Home after the browser has a valid remembered location. Optional launch-key shortcuts can be disabled without removing the Applications and Last folder navigation shortcuts. Files and device discovery Files is a persistent browser tab. Tools > Essentials > File Browser switches to this same tab and resumes its folder and selection. The list displays eleven rows above the status line and footer hints. The root lets you select devices and start lazy drivers; Not checked is not the same as absent. The old separate Devices landing view and Files landing page are not additional visible tabs. Asynchronous USB reads can complete after a tab change. Input remains guarded until outstanding reads finish, rather than treating a tab switch as permission to poll PAD immediately. Built-in command targets Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. The popup file browser for Settings > Launching > Automatic start & boot > Automatic start target and Launch keys > Choose target retains MISC/ for selecting built-in commands. Configure switches to Settings, and File Browser switches to Files. Tools cautions Long caution messages in Tools scroll upward slowly inside the warning box so the entire message can be read. The text pauses at the end for two seconds, then restarts from the beginning. Selecting another tool restarts its caution text. Messages that fit remain still. Navigation L1/R1 cycles Home, Files, Tools and Settings. File Browser supports context actions and multi-selection. The visible section count is four even though compatibility code retains older internal section identifiers. Status and visuals Clock, notifications, confirmation toasts, animation, BGM, screen calibration and themes have explicit display settings. Dashboard double buffering reduces horizontal scanout tearing, but passing CI does not establish every console/display combination is flicker-free. See Themes & BGM and Troubleshooting ."},{"title":"Four top-level tabs","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#tabs","text":"Tab Behavior in current builds Home Continue carousel, pinned/recent/favorite applications, Applications library shortcut, Last folder shortcut when available, and optional configured launch-key shortcuts. Files Opens the native File Browser directly inside the dashboard frame. Browse drives and folders, operate on files and return to the same folder/selection after switching tabs. Tools File Browser, Text Editor, USB unit diagnostics, HDD Manager, exploit installer, individual built-in and network tools. Settings Appearance, Home/app library, sound, input, launching, devices/MMCE, network, and system settings."},{"title":"Home and Applications","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#home-apps","text":"Select Applications in Home > Browse & shortcuts to enter the full library without adding a fifth top-level tab. When Recent applications is set to 0 and no recent history is saved, entering Home opens this viewer automatically. Back returns to Browse & shortcuts. Selecting Applications as the Start screen opens the full library with All selected. The library keeps its Grid/List/Carousel views, favorites, pins, filtering and recents. SELECT cycles application views. Add or remove a favorite from the application options; the result also displays a toast. Last folder appears on Home after the browser has a valid remembered location. Optional launch-key shortcuts can be disabled without removing the Applications and Last folder navigation shortcuts."},{"title":"Files and device discovery","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#files","text":"Files is a persistent browser tab. Tools > Essentials > File Browser switches to this same tab and resumes its folder and selection. The list displays eleven rows above the status line and footer hints. The root lets you select devices and start lazy drivers; Not checked is not the same as absent. The old separate Devices landing view and Files landing page are not additional visible tabs. Asynchronous USB reads can complete after a tab change. Input remains guarded until outstanding reads finish, rather than treating a tab switch as permission to poll PAD immediately."},{"title":"Built-in command targets","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#misc-commands","text":"Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. The popup file browser for Settings > Launching > Automatic start & boot > Automatic start target and Launch keys > Choose target retains MISC/ for selecting built-in commands. Configure switches to Settings, and File Browser switches to Files."},{"title":"Tools cautions","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#tools-cautions","text":"Long caution messages in Tools scroll upward slowly inside the warning box so the entire message can be read. The text pauses at the end for two seconds, then restarts from the beginning. Selecting another tool restarts its caution text. Messages that fit remain still."},{"title":"Navigation","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#navigation","text":"L1/R1 cycles Home, Files, Tools and Settings. File Browser supports context actions and multi-selection. The visible section count is four even though compatibility code retains older internal section identifiers."},{"title":"Status and visuals","cat":"interface","url":"dashboard.html#display","text":"Clock, notifications, confirmation toasts, animation, BGM, screen calibration and themes have explicit display settings. Dashboard double buffering reduces horizontal scanout tearing, but passing CI does not establish every console/display combination is flicker-free. See Themes & BGM and Troubleshooting ."},{"title":"File Browser","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html","text":"File Browser Files is a first-class, persistent dashboard tab using the native browser. Tools > Essentials > File Browser opens this same tab. Eleven rows fit above the status line and footer hints; classic wLaunchELF file operations remain supported through the shared backends. Launching BIN/CUE, VCD or ISO game images does not add them to recent applications or schedule the shared favorites/history save. Recent applications set to 0 also prevents recording application launches. Tab and folder continuity Files opens directly into the browser without a separate landing page. L1/R1 switches top-level dashboard tabs; returning preserves the browser folder and selection. The Home tab provides the Last folder shortcut. Built-in utilities have individual entries under Tools. Opening files .PNG , .JPG , .JPEG — open in the native dashboard image viewer. PNG uses the existing bounded libpng decoder; JPEG uses a bounded libjpeg path derived from the historical wLaunchELF JPG support. Image viewer controls: Left/Right browse other PNG/JPG/JPEG images in the current sorted folder (wrapping at either end), Up/Down rotate 90 degrees, L2/R2 zoom, left stick pans while zoomed, Square toggles fullscreen, Start toggles slideshow, L1/R1 changes the 1-60 second slideshow delay, Select resets the view, and Confirm/Back closes the viewer. Slideshow uses the same mixed-format folder order. .ELF , .XLF , .KELF — executable launch path. .VCD — POPStarter PS1 handoff. .CUE , .BIN , PS1 .EXE — Ember when the image is directly inside <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ . The umbrella name is not fixed. .ISO — Neutrino PS2 handoff. Text/config formats — dashboard Text Editor. Folders — enter folder. Ember handoff USB Ember launches use a clean IOP handoff. Launchstation finishes background I/O and audio, resets the IOP, and rebuilds only the USB filesystem stack before starting Ember. The launcher deliberately does not reload its homebrew SIO2MAN/PADMAN modules; Ember loads the ROM pad modules itself. This avoids the hardware-observed pad init rc=-1 conflict while keeping the selected USB game tree mounted again before Ember starts. Operations Triangle opens contextual operations. Depending on device and file type, the browser can copy, cut, paste, rename, delete, create folders, calculate size, edit or organize timestamps, work with PSU saves, mount VMC images, edit text or invoke specialist tools. Naming files and folders New-folder, rename and Save As dialogs use the Text Editor's compact character layout and wide Space key. Symbols, Tab, Return and on-screen Del are omitted from naming dialogs; the controller delete shortcut remains available. Forward slash ( / ) and backslash ( \\ ) are hidden and cannot be typed with a USB keyboard. START accepts the name; Triangle cancels and restores the original text. Naming dialogs accept one line of text. Selection Square marks entries. Operations apply to the marked set; otherwise they apply to the focused entry. In the operations menu, Select all uses a checkmark with a small plus at the upper left, and Clear selection uses the matching checkmark with a minus at the upper left. Invert selection uses a filled rounded square with the entire central checkmark drawn as negative space. Classic exploit-protection checks remain for sensitive memory-card paths. System-update folder warnings name the target region: BIEXEC-SYSTEM is Japan, BAEXEC-SYSTEM is America/Asia, BEEXEC-SYSTEM is Europe, and BCEXEC-SYSTEM is China. Deleting or modifying an item inside one of these folders shows the same region warning. Clipboard source After Copy or Cut, the footer shows the matching Copy or Cut icon followed by the full source path for a single file or folder. Multiple selections show the icon followed by Assortment in <folder path> . The icon uses exactly the same color as the text, including when a theme applies a separate icon tint. Files and folders in the clipboard show outlined scissors for Cut or overlapping rounded squares for Copy in their source directory, including after returning to that folder. Clipboard icons and filenames keep the marked-selection color until the clipboard is cleared or replaced, including after navigating away and returning. The same symbols appear in the Cut and Copy menu entries. Paste with new names uses a pencil with a small plus in its upper-right corner. Marking an item shows its checkmark even if it is already in the clipboard. Unmarking it reveals its Cut or Copy symbol again while it remains in the clipboard. Long paths scroll within the available status line. The source stays visible when browsing to another destination; loaded launch arguments retain priority in that line. Copy, move and paste progress The transfer popup shows Pasting file X of N , the current filename, a rounded progress bar, copied bytes at its left and the current file's total size at its right, then average speed and remaining time. Both size labels use the same spacing below the bar as tab labels use below their icons. The speed and time rows move together with them. Both size labels use the same unit, chosen from the current file's total size: KB below 1 GB, MB below 1 TB, and GB for larger files (1024-based units). Amounts below 1 KB show fractional KB; larger KB amounts use whole numbers, such as 1200 KB. MB and GB retain two decimal places. The copied amount increases as data is written. Long filenames are shortened to fit the popup; the original names and paths are used for the transfer. Launchstation counts files before starting, including files inside selected folders and payload files inside PSU archives; folders and the PSU dot entries do not count as files. PSU creation and restoration use the same layout. The bar and time estimate apply to the current file, while average speed covers the whole paste operation. The retained R3Z 4.80 rate and time calculations are unchanged. Current speed: bytes written since the previous progress sample, multiplied by 1000 and divided by the elapsed milliseconds. Remaining time: after at least one second has elapsed since the previous sample, measure bytes written over that interval. The first valid sample initializes the estimate; later samples use (3 * previous rate + new rate) / 4 . Divide the current file's remaining bytes by this smoothed rate, rounded down to whole seconds. No estimate is shown until the first valid sample; a sampled stall shows Unknown until data resumes. Average speed: total copied bytes divided by the elapsed time since the paste operation started. This includes setup and other time between files. Units: speeds use B/s or KB/s (1024 bytes per KB). Long estimates show hours, minutes and seconds. Verification and deletion use their own progress displays without stale transfer rates. The rounded progress bar remains bounded to its track. Sorting The dashboard browser's Sort by popup lists Type, Name, Game title, Size, Date-Time, Extension, As stored in that order. Type is the startup default, including when no configuration file is found. A changed sort choice lasts for the current session. Extension sorts by filename suffix. Type keeps four groups in order: folders; ELF executables (including XLF and KELF); recognized text/config files; then all remaining files, including game images. Folders and executables use ascending filename order. Within the text/config and remaining-file groups, files are grouped alphabetically by extension, then alphabetically by filename within each extension. Matching is case-insensitive, including numbered names, with no direction prompt. Files without an extension lead the final group. Other sorted modes can be Ascending or Descending, with folders always first. Classic File Browser: four display modes The classic L1 mode menu now supports four upstream-compatible display modes: File names , File names + details , Game titles and Game titles + details . Circle switches between filename modes, while Square switches between title modes; each repeated press toggles the detail column. The game-title modes use existing title metadata and the Shift-JIS renderer, while the two no-details modes use the full row width. Filename/title sorting and ascending/descending controls remain independent. The dashboard Files tab and its modal browser also provide filename/title modes with optional details. These display controls do not start a device scan or change storage initialization. Classic file-browser improvements (October 2026) The classic modal File Browser's L1 display/sort menu now supports reversible sorting: choose Filename (D-pad Down), Game title (Left) or Timestamp (Up); press the same direction again to reverse its order. Right selects No sort . Triangle returns to the browser. Folders remain ahead of files and ELF entries ahead of other files in both directions. The dashboard Files tab has its own independent sort selector, including the fixed-order Type grouping described above. In memory-card save titles, Launchstation now respects icon.sys 's title line-break offset for PS2 save folders and PSU archives, adding a separator without splitting Shift-JIS characters or duplicating existing spaces. The *Tuna Date operation for a selected real MC folder is now inside Folder dates... , with Manual, A-Z, Z-A and SAS ordering. It is deliberately absent when the selection is not an eligible physical memory-card folder; the older HDD/PFS date functions are unchanged. Size and date-time Size and date-time occupy independent columns with an explicit gutter. Seconds are retained. Filename width is calculated from actual metadata width so full timestamps fit rather than being shortened. Root devices Root entries use two left-aligned columns: the device path on the left and its description on the right. The Files tab and file/folder picker share this layout. USB entries appear immediately after Memory Card Slot 2. For example, mc0:/ shows Memory Card Slot 1 , mmce0:/ shows SD2PSX SD Card Slot 1 , and usbN:/ shows USB Storage . The list also covers both APA and exFAT HDD units, MX4SIO, CDVD, UDPFS, PC Host, PSX DVR, PSX Internal Flash ( xfrom0:/ ), compatible PC CARD CF adapters, and mounted virtual memory cards. The automatic-start and launch-key target pickers retain a separate MISC/ entry for built-in commands. In the File Browser, device startup shows its operation title in the path bar where Choose a device or the current folder normally appears, without a progress popup. Internal memory-card synchronization and per-unit probe details stay hidden. A message is shown if no USB storage is found. Diagnostic status remains available in device tools. Current-folder paths in the browser and folder picker use numbered usbN:/ labels, including when the saved path uses mass:/ or massN:/ . Display formatting does not change file operations or ELF handoffs. USB discovery does not assume a permanent mass0: . Rows and navigation The native browser and path picker show ten rows within the dashboard safe area, with centered scrolling where the list permits it. Up/Down wraps one item at a time. Left/Right pages to an endpoint before wrapping on the next press; subsequent presses resume paging. The classic browser retains its display-dependent capacity and existing endpoint-first paging."},{"title":"Tab and folder continuity","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#tabs","text":"Files opens directly into the browser without a separate landing page. L1/R1 switches top-level dashboard tabs; returning preserves the browser folder and selection. The Home tab provides the Last folder shortcut. Built-in utilities have individual entries under Tools."},{"title":"Opening files","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#open","text":".PNG , .JPG , .JPEG — open in the native dashboard image viewer. PNG uses the existing bounded libpng decoder; JPEG uses a bounded libjpeg path derived from the historical wLaunchELF JPG support. Image viewer controls: Left/Right browse other PNG/JPG/JPEG images in the current sorted folder (wrapping at either end), Up/Down rotate 90 degrees, L2/R2 zoom, left stick pans while zoomed, Square toggles fullscreen, Start toggles slideshow, L1/R1 changes the 1-60 second slideshow delay, Select resets the view, and Confirm/Back closes the viewer. Slideshow uses the same mixed-format folder order. .ELF , .XLF , .KELF — executable launch path. .VCD — POPStarter PS1 handoff. .CUE , .BIN , PS1 .EXE — Ember when the image is directly inside <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ . The umbrella name is not fixed. .ISO — Neutrino PS2 handoff. Text/config formats — dashboard Text Editor. Folders — enter folder."},{"title":"Ember handoff","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#ember-handoff","text":"USB Ember launches use a clean IOP handoff. Launchstation finishes background I/O and audio, resets the IOP, and rebuilds only the USB filesystem stack before starting Ember. The launcher deliberately does not reload its homebrew SIO2MAN/PADMAN modules; Ember loads the ROM pad modules itself. This avoids the hardware-observed pad init rc=-1 conflict while keeping the selected USB game tree mounted again before Ember starts."},{"title":"Operations","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#operations","text":"Triangle opens contextual operations. Depending on device and file type, the browser can copy, cut, paste, rename, delete, create folders, calculate size, edit or organize timestamps, work with PSU saves, mount VMC images, edit text or invoke specialist tools."},{"title":"Naming files and folders","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#naming","text":"New-folder, rename and Save As dialogs use the Text Editor's compact character layout and wide Space key. Symbols, Tab, Return and on-screen Del are omitted from naming dialogs; the controller delete shortcut remains available. Forward slash ( / ) and backslash ( \\ ) are hidden and cannot be typed with a USB keyboard. START accepts the name; Triangle cancels and restores the original text. Naming dialogs accept one line of text."},{"title":"Selection","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#selection","text":"Square marks entries. Operations apply to the marked set; otherwise they apply to the focused entry. In the operations menu, Select all uses a checkmark with a small plus at the upper left, and Clear selection uses the matching checkmark with a minus at the upper left. Invert selection uses a filled rounded square with the entire central checkmark drawn as negative space. Classic exploit-protection checks remain for sensitive memory-card paths. System-update folder warnings name the target region: BIEXEC-SYSTEM is Japan, BAEXEC-SYSTEM is America/Asia, BEEXEC-SYSTEM is Europe, and BCEXEC-SYSTEM is China. Deleting or modifying an item inside one of these folders shows the same region warning."},{"title":"Clipboard source","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#clipboard","text":"After Copy or Cut, the footer shows the matching Copy or Cut icon followed by the full source path for a single file or folder. Multiple selections show the icon followed by Assortment in <folder path> . The icon uses exactly the same color as the text, including when a theme applies a separate icon tint. Files and folders in the clipboard show outlined scissors for Cut or overlapping rounded squares for Copy in their source directory, including after returning to that folder. Clipboard icons and filenames keep the marked-selection color until the clipboard is cleared or replaced, including after navigating away and returning. The same symbols appear in the Cut and Copy menu entries. Paste with new names uses a pencil with a small plus in its upper-right corner. Marking an item shows its checkmark even if it is already in the clipboard. Unmarking it reveals its Cut or Copy symbol again while it remains in the clipboard. Long paths scroll within the available status line. The source stays visible when browsing to another destination; loaded launch arguments retain priority in that line."},{"title":"Copy, move and paste progress","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#transfer-progress","text":"The transfer popup shows Pasting file X of N , the current filename, a rounded progress bar, copied bytes at its left and the current file's total size at its right, then average speed and remaining time. Both size labels use the same spacing below the bar as tab labels use below their icons. The speed and time rows move together with them. Both size labels use the same unit, chosen from the current file's total size: KB below 1 GB, MB below 1 TB, and GB for larger files (1024-based units). Amounts below 1 KB show fractional KB; larger KB amounts use whole numbers, such as 1200 KB. MB and GB retain two decimal places. The copied amount increases as data is written. Long filenames are shortened to fit the popup; the original names and paths are used for the transfer. Launchstation counts files before starting, including files inside selected folders and payload files inside PSU archives; folders and the PSU dot entries do not count as files. PSU creation and restoration use the same layout. The bar and time estimate apply to the current file, while average speed covers the whole paste operation. The retained R3Z 4.80 rate and time calculations are unchanged. Current speed: bytes written since the previous progress sample, multiplied by 1000 and divided by the elapsed milliseconds. Remaining time: after at least one second has elapsed since the previous sample, measure bytes written over that interval. The first valid sample initializes the estimate; later samples use (3 * previous rate + new rate) / 4 . Divide the current file's remaining bytes by this smoothed rate, rounded down to whole seconds. No estimate is shown until the first valid sample; a sampled stall shows Unknown until data resumes. Average speed: total copied bytes divided by the elapsed time since the paste operation started. This includes setup and other time between files. Units: speeds use B/s or KB/s (1024 bytes per KB). Long estimates show hours, minutes and seconds. Verification and deletion use their own progress displays without stale transfer rates. The rounded progress bar remains bounded to its track."},{"title":"Sorting","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#sorting","text":"The dashboard browser's Sort by popup lists Type, Name, Game title, Size, Date-Time, Extension, As stored in that order. Type is the startup default, including when no configuration file is found. A changed sort choice lasts for the current session. Extension sorts by filename suffix. Type keeps four groups in order: folders; ELF executables (including XLF and KELF); recognized text/config files; then all remaining files, including game images. Folders and executables use ascending filename order. Within the text/config and remaining-file groups, files are grouped alphabetically by extension, then alphabetically by filename within each extension. Matching is case-insensitive, including numbered names, with no direction prompt. Files without an extension lead the final group. Other sorted modes can be Ascending or Descending, with folders always first."},{"title":"Classic File Browser: four display modes","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#display-modes","text":"The classic L1 mode menu now supports four upstream-compatible display modes: File names , File names + details , Game titles and Game titles + details . Circle switches between filename modes, while Square switches between title modes; each repeated press toggles the detail column. The game-title modes use existing title metadata and the Shift-JIS renderer, while the two no-details modes use the full row width. Filename/title sorting and ascending/descending controls remain independent. The dashboard Files tab and its modal browser also provide filename/title modes with optional details. These display controls do not start a device scan or change storage initialization."},{"title":"Classic file-browser improvements (October 2026)","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#upstream-r3z","text":"The classic modal File Browser's L1 display/sort menu now supports reversible sorting: choose Filename (D-pad Down), Game title (Left) or Timestamp (Up); press the same direction again to reverse its order. Right selects No sort . Triangle returns to the browser. Folders remain ahead of files and ELF entries ahead of other files in both directions. The dashboard Files tab has its own independent sort selector, including the fixed-order Type grouping described above. In memory-card save titles, Launchstation now respects icon.sys 's title line-break offset for PS2 save folders and PSU archives, adding a separator without splitting Shift-JIS characters or duplicating existing spaces. The *Tuna Date operation for a selected real MC folder is now inside Folder dates... , with Manual, A-Z, Z-A and SAS ordering. It is deliberately absent when the selection is not an eligible physical memory-card folder; the older HDD/PFS date functions are unchanged."},{"title":"Size and date-time","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#metadata","text":"Size and date-time occupy independent columns with an explicit gutter. Seconds are retained. Filename width is calculated from actual metadata width so full timestamps fit rather than being shortened."},{"title":"Root devices","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#root","text":"Root entries use two left-aligned columns: the device path on the left and its description on the right. The Files tab and file/folder picker share this layout. USB entries appear immediately after Memory Card Slot 2. For example, mc0:/ shows Memory Card Slot 1 , mmce0:/ shows SD2PSX SD Card Slot 1 , and usbN:/ shows USB Storage . The list also covers both APA and exFAT HDD units, MX4SIO, CDVD, UDPFS, PC Host, PSX DVR, PSX Internal Flash ( xfrom0:/ ), compatible PC CARD CF adapters, and mounted virtual memory cards. The automatic-start and launch-key target pickers retain a separate MISC/ entry for built-in commands. In the File Browser, device startup shows its operation title in the path bar where Choose a device or the current folder normally appears, without a progress popup. Internal memory-card synchronization and per-unit probe details stay hidden. A message is shown if no USB storage is found. Diagnostic status remains available in device tools. Current-folder paths in the browser and folder picker use numbered usbN:/ labels, including when the saved path uses mass:/ or massN:/ . Display formatting does not change file operations or ELF handoffs. USB discovery does not assume a permanent mass0: ."},{"title":"Rows and navigation","cat":"interface","url":"file-browser.html#paging","text":"The native browser and path picker show ten rows within the dashboard safe area, with centered scrolling where the list permits it. Up/Down wraps one item at a time. Left/Right pages to an endpoint before wrapping on the next press; subsequent presses resume paging. The classic browser retains its display-dependent capacity and existing endpoint-first paging."},{"title":"Applications","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html","text":"Applications Library Applications is the full library nested under Home, not an extra top-level tab. Use Home > Applications to browse configured folders, favorites, pinned apps and recents. Home integration The Continue carousel surfaces pinned, recent and favorite applications; Last folder is available after a valid path has been visited. SELECT cycles Grid, List and Carousel in the Applications library. Filters include All, All including hidden, Favorites and device categories. Choosing Applications as the Start screen opens All; ordinary visits keep the saved filter. During a scan, the viewer shows Looking for applications. If cached applications are on devices that are not ready, it says so instead of showing an empty Favorites message. What is scanned Only configured Application folders are scanned. Defaults: mc:/APPS/ mc:/BOOT/ mass:/APPS/ mass:/BOOT/ Each configured root includes one subfolder level. This avoids silently crawling every attached filesystem. Manage scan roots Open Settings → Home & applications → Application library → Application folders... . Each current root is labeled “this folder + one subfolder.” Add Folder chooses an exact root; Restore Defaults restores the standard memory-card and mass roots. Presentation The dashboard can show application metadata and artwork when present. Long titles use bounded marquee behavior. Unfocused app rendering has stable alpha/scissor state so memory-card-style icons do not become half rendered. Artwork & covers Every application gets a default tile : a generated icon tinted per app. Its style is set in Settings > Customize theme > Icons > Default app icon . To show your own cover instead, add a PNG in one of the places below. Settings > Home & applications > Application artwork must be On. Image format PNG only (transparency is supported). Any size up to 1.5 MB per file. Images are scaled down to fit 128×128 and never enlarged, so square art of 128×128 or more looks best. Interlaced PNGs are limited to 256×256. Where Launchstation looks For an app at mass:/APPS/OPL/OPNPS2LD.ELF , the first file found wins: A cover = line in the app's metadata sidecar (see below). Beside the ELF: OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png , OPNPS2LD_COV.png or OPNPS2LD.png . In the app's own folder (not a scan root such as mass:/APPS/ itself): cover.png or icon.png . Beside the ELF: OPNPS2LD_ICO.png (OPL disc-icon naming). An ART folder at the root of the same device, using OPL naming: mass:/ART/OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png or mass:/ART/OPNPS2LD_COV.png (the +OPL partition on an internal HDD). An ART folder in Launchstation's settings folder (beside DASHBOARD.CNF ): OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png , OPNPS2LD_COV.png or OPNPS2LD.png . Use this for apps on devices that are not started yet. Covers prepared for OPL's ART folder work as-is. Choosing a cover in metadata Use Application options > Edit metadata from Home or Applications to open title.cfg beside the ELF. If it is missing, Launchstation offers to create it with the app title and ELF filename. It uses plain key = value text: title = Open PS2 Loader boot = OPNPS2LD.ELF cover = covers/opl.png A relative cover path is resolved from the ELF's folder; a full path such as mc0:/ART/opl.png also works. art and icon are accepted as synonyms. Existing ELF-specific sidecars such as OPNPS2LD.cfg are still read by the library. After adding art Choose Refresh library (Applications options) or Settings > Home & applications > Application library > Refresh library now . Covers already found are kept, and apps that had none are checked again. Saving from Edit metadata re-reads that one app immediately. Favorites and app metadata Application options can add/remove Favorites, pin a shortcut, edit metadata and show the app in File Browser. Favorite changes show a toast. In the Applications Options menu, Hide from view sits above Refresh library . Hidden applications are omitted from Home and normal library filters; their ELFs, favorites and pins are kept. The choice survives refreshes and restarts. To restore one, choose Show applications > All including hidden (directly below All), select it, then choose Show in view . Hidden paths are saved in DASHAPPS.CNF , independently of the library cache. Launching ELFs use the shared loader path. Optional argument sidecars and the Text Editor's “Use as launch arguments” action can provide custom argv."},{"title":"Home integration","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#home","text":"The Continue carousel surfaces pinned, recent and favorite applications; Last folder is available after a valid path has been visited. SELECT cycles Grid, List and Carousel in the Applications library. Filters include All, All including hidden, Favorites and device categories. Choosing Applications as the Start screen opens All; ordinary visits keep the saved filter. During a scan, the viewer shows Looking for applications. If cached applications are on devices that are not ready, it says so instead of showing an empty Favorites message."},{"title":"What is scanned","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#scope","text":"Only configured Application folders are scanned. Defaults: mc:/APPS/ mc:/BOOT/ mass:/APPS/ mass:/BOOT/ Each configured root includes one subfolder level. This avoids silently crawling every attached filesystem."},{"title":"Manage scan roots","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#manage","text":"Open Settings → Home & applications → Application library → Application folders... . Each current root is labeled “this folder + one subfolder.” Add Folder chooses an exact root; Restore Defaults restores the standard memory-card and mass roots."},{"title":"Presentation","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#presentation","text":"The dashboard can show application metadata and artwork when present. Long titles use bounded marquee behavior. Unfocused app rendering has stable alpha/scissor state so memory-card-style icons do not become half rendered."},{"title":"Artwork & covers","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#artwork","text":"Every application gets a default tile : a generated icon tinted per app. Its style is set in Settings > Customize theme > Icons > Default app icon . To show your own cover instead, add a PNG in one of the places below. Settings > Home & applications > Application artwork must be On. Image format PNG only (transparency is supported). Any size up to 1.5 MB per file. Images are scaled down to fit 128×128 and never enlarged, so square art of 128×128 or more looks best. Interlaced PNGs are limited to 256×256. Where Launchstation looks For an app at mass:/APPS/OPL/OPNPS2LD.ELF , the first file found wins: A cover = line in the app's metadata sidecar (see below). Beside the ELF: OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png , OPNPS2LD_COV.png or OPNPS2LD.png . In the app's own folder (not a scan root such as mass:/APPS/ itself): cover.png or icon.png . Beside the ELF: OPNPS2LD_ICO.png (OPL disc-icon naming). An ART folder at the root of the same device, using OPL naming: mass:/ART/OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png or mass:/ART/OPNPS2LD_COV.png (the +OPL partition on an internal HDD). An ART folder in Launchstation's settings folder (beside DASHBOARD.CNF ): OPNPS2LD.ELF_COV.png , OPNPS2LD_COV.png or OPNPS2LD.png . Use this for apps on devices that are not started yet. Covers prepared for OPL's ART folder work as-is. Choosing a cover in metadata Use Application options > Edit metadata from Home or Applications to open title.cfg beside the ELF. If it is missing, Launchstation offers to create it with the app title and ELF filename. It uses plain key = value text: title = Open PS2 Loader boot = OPNPS2LD.ELF cover = covers/opl.png A relative cover path is resolved from the ELF's folder; a full path such as mc0:/ART/opl.png also works. art and icon are accepted as synonyms. Existing ELF-specific sidecars such as OPNPS2LD.cfg are still read by the library. After adding art Choose Refresh library (Applications options) or Settings > Home & applications > Application library > Refresh library now . Covers already found are kept, and apps that had none are checked again. Saving from Edit metadata re-reads that one app immediately."},{"title":"Favorites and app metadata","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#favorites","text":"Application options can add/remove Favorites, pin a shortcut, edit metadata and show the app in File Browser. Favorite changes show a toast. In the Applications Options menu, Hide from view sits above Refresh library . Hidden applications are omitted from Home and normal library filters; their ELFs, favorites and pins are kept. The choice survives refreshes and restarts. To restore one, choose Show applications > All including hidden (directly below All), select it, then choose Show in view . Hidden paths are saved in DASHAPPS.CNF , independently of the library cache."},{"title":"Launching","cat":"interface","url":"apps.html#launch","text":"ELFs use the shared loader path. Optional argument sidecars and the Text Editor's “Use as launch arguments” action can provide custom argv."},{"title":"Text Editor","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html","text":"Text Editor A dashboard-native editor for configuration, scripts, argument lists and other text files. Typical files CNF, CFG, TXT, INI, CHT, PBT, JS, LUA, XML, TOML, YAML, YML and ARG files can be opened from File Browser. Navigation D-pad Move L1/R1 Line start/end L2/R2 Page Square Select Triangle Options The full current path is shown beneath the document title and scrolls when necessary. With the keyboard hidden, 18 lines of text fit above the footer; with it shown, 10 lines remain visible. Footer hints are intentionally compact enough for the PS2 safe width. Editor menu Save, Save As, New, Open, Switch Document, Start/Stop Selection, Select All, Copy, Cut, Paste, Line Endings, Go to Start, Go to End, Use as launch arguments, Close and Exit are available from Triangle > Options, including while the virtual keyboard is shown. Document text follows the selected GUI font and Bold setting while keeping fixed-width columns. Special byte glyphs and the System font retain the active bitmap font. On-screen and USB keyboards Confirm opens the virtual keyboard. SELECT hides/shows it. The wide Space key inserts a space and, on QWERTY, spans from the left edge of C to the right edge of period. In both the editor and naming keyboards, reversing the move into Space restores the previously selected key, even after inserting several spaces. While the keyboard is open, use the D-pad to select keys and tilt the right stick to move the text cursor in all four directions. L1/R1 also moves the cursor left/right, and L2/R2 moves it up/down. Tab and Return use the familiar keyboard arrow symbols, drawn independently of the selected font. They sit in a right-side column on rows 1 and 2, with Symbols on row 5. The side buttons are right-aligned, with spacing that fits beside the main character grid. On QWERTY, backslash/pipe sits immediately after apostrophe/double quote. Return starts a new line. Delete uses the controller button, with no duplicate on-screen key. Choose Symbols repeatedly to cycle through four-row pages of punctuation and extended characters with the same column count as the chosen letter layout, then return to your configured keyboard layout. Its label stays Symbols on every page. Both the editor and naming keyboards align the center of column 7 with the screen center, independently of the side buttons. Switching pages keeps the grid width, Tab, Return and Space in the same positions. The editor keyboard sits lower to show 11 editable text rows above it, with a gap before the footer hints. The shared layout scales with the selected video mode. File and folder naming dialogs, including Save As, keep the same key arrangement and Space key, with Symbols, Tab and Return hidden. Naming keyboards use the same column-7 alignment; hidden actions do not reserve space. The question-mark and pipe keys stay visible and type ? and | with Caps either off or on. Slash and backslash ( / and \\ ) are hidden and also rejected from USB keyboard input when entering names. In naming dialogs, tilt the right stick left/right to move the text cursor while the D-pad selects keys. Naming popups show the title, destination folder path, typing area and keyboard in that order. Long folder paths scroll within their own line. The button helpers span the popup width with even gaps between groups and labels vertically centered on their icons. START accepts a name; the controller delete shortcut remains available. Square toggles Caps, and Cancel deletes the previous character. In both keyboards, holding Type or Delete repeats with the same initial delay and acceleration as list scrolling. USB keyboard input is supported when enabled and its driver is available. Launch with arguments Write one argument per line and choose Use as launch arguments . The editor validates the buffer and hands argv to the same loader used elsewhere."},{"title":"Typical files","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html#formats","text":"CNF, CFG, TXT, INI, CHT, PBT, JS, LUA, XML, TOML, YAML, YML and ARG files can be opened from File Browser."},{"title":"Navigation","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html#navigation","text":"D-pad Move L1/R1 Line start/end L2/R2 Page Square Select Triangle Options The full current path is shown beneath the document title and scrolls when necessary. With the keyboard hidden, 18 lines of text fit above the footer; with it shown, 10 lines remain visible. Footer hints are intentionally compact enough for the PS2 safe width."},{"title":"Editor menu","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html#menu","text":"Save, Save As, New, Open, Switch Document, Start/Stop Selection, Select All, Copy, Cut, Paste, Line Endings, Go to Start, Go to End, Use as launch arguments, Close and Exit are available from Triangle > Options, including while the virtual keyboard is shown. Document text follows the selected GUI font and Bold setting while keeping fixed-width columns. Special byte glyphs and the System font retain the active bitmap font."},{"title":"On-screen and USB keyboards","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html#keyboard","text":"Confirm opens the virtual keyboard. SELECT hides/shows it. The wide Space key inserts a space and, on QWERTY, spans from the left edge of C to the right edge of period. In both the editor and naming keyboards, reversing the move into Space restores the previously selected key, even after inserting several spaces. While the keyboard is open, use the D-pad to select keys and tilt the right stick to move the text cursor in all four directions. L1/R1 also moves the cursor left/right, and L2/R2 moves it up/down. Tab and Return use the familiar keyboard arrow symbols, drawn independently of the selected font. They sit in a right-side column on rows 1 and 2, with Symbols on row 5. The side buttons are right-aligned, with spacing that fits beside the main character grid. On QWERTY, backslash/pipe sits immediately after apostrophe/double quote. Return starts a new line. Delete uses the controller button, with no duplicate on-screen key. Choose Symbols repeatedly to cycle through four-row pages of punctuation and extended characters with the same column count as the chosen letter layout, then return to your configured keyboard layout. Its label stays Symbols on every page. Both the editor and naming keyboards align the center of column 7 with the screen center, independently of the side buttons. Switching pages keeps the grid width, Tab, Return and Space in the same positions. The editor keyboard sits lower to show 11 editable text rows above it, with a gap before the footer hints. The shared layout scales with the selected video mode. File and folder naming dialogs, including Save As, keep the same key arrangement and Space key, with Symbols, Tab and Return hidden. Naming keyboards use the same column-7 alignment; hidden actions do not reserve space. The question-mark and pipe keys stay visible and type ? and | with Caps either off or on. Slash and backslash ( / and \\ ) are hidden and also rejected from USB keyboard input when entering names. In naming dialogs, tilt the right stick left/right to move the text cursor while the D-pad selects keys. Naming popups show the title, destination folder path, typing area and keyboard in that order. Long folder paths scroll within their own line. The button helpers span the popup width with even gaps between groups and labels vertically centered on their icons. START accepts a name; the controller delete shortcut remains availa"},{"title":"Launch with arguments","cat":"interface","url":"text-editor.html#launch-args","text":"Write one argument per line and choose Use as launch arguments . The editor validates the buffer and hands argv to the same loader used elsewhere."},{"title":"Launch Arguments","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html","text":"Launch Arguments Launchstation can cache a user argument list, consume a sidecar .arg file, or launch directly from a Text Editor buffer. File Browser workflow Select a Text Editor-supported argument file. Open its operations menu and choose Use as launch arguments ( Launch ELF with Args in the classic browser). The action is dimmed and cannot be selected for folders or files that cannot be opened in the Text Editor, or when the device does not support this action. Launchstation parses and caches the argument lines in memory. Select the target ELF. The cached arguments are appended after the normal handoff path. Text Editor workflow The Text Editor offers the same action for the current in-memory buffer. Unsaved edits are used, which makes it convenient for testing launch flags without repeatedly writing the argument file. Automatic sidecar arguments For an ordinary ELF launch, Launchstation checks for a same-directory sidecar whose base name matches the ELF, for example: APPS/ ├── BOOT.ELF └── BOOT.arg If arguments were already cached manually through the browser/editor action, the sidecar is ignored for that launch. Format and limits Rule Current limit One non-empty line One user argument User arguments 12 lines Characters per argument 255 Total cached argument text 2,048 bytes including terminators Line endings are stripped. Other characters on each line are preserved. argv[0] The target executable still receives the normal selected/handoff path as argv[0] ; cached user lines follow it. External-core paths such as Neutrino build their own controlled argument set and do not consume a stray normal-ELF argument cache."},{"title":"File Browser workflow","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html#file-browser","text":"Select a Text Editor-supported argument file. Open its operations menu and choose Use as launch arguments ( Launch ELF with Args in the classic browser). The action is dimmed and cannot be selected for folders or files that cannot be opened in the Text Editor, or when the device does not support this action. Launchstation parses and caches the argument lines in memory. Select the target ELF. The cached arguments are appended after the normal handoff path."},{"title":"Text Editor workflow","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html#editor","text":"The Text Editor offers the same action for the current in-memory buffer. Unsaved edits are used, which makes it convenient for testing launch flags without repeatedly writing the argument file."},{"title":"Automatic sidecar arguments","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html#sidecar","text":"For an ordinary ELF launch, Launchstation checks for a same-directory sidecar whose base name matches the ELF, for example: APPS/ ├── BOOT.ELF └── BOOT.arg If arguments were already cached manually through the browser/editor action, the sidecar is ignored for that launch."},{"title":"Format and limits","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html#format","text":"Rule Current limit One non-empty line One user argument User arguments 12 lines Characters per argument 255 Total cached argument text 2,048 bytes including terminators Line endings are stripped. Other characters on each line are preserved."},{"title":"argv[0]","cat":"interface","url":"launch-arguments.html#argv0","text":"The target executable still receives the normal selected/handoff path as argv[0] ; cached user lines follow it. External-core paths such as Neutrino build their own controlled argument set and do not consume a stray normal-ELF argument cache."},{"title":"Controls","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html","text":"Controls Button labels follow configured confirm/cancel mapping, so the face button can differ by region or preference. Confirmation popups use the displayed face buttons directly: Confirm performs the named action, and Back/Cancel dismisses it. For example, Confirm clears recent applications and Cancel keeps the list. D-pad input does not select an action, and there is no selection highlight. Dashboard Control Action Confirm Open focused item, device or page. Back Close or return. L1 / R1 Previous / next top-level section. SELECT in Home > Applications Cycle Grid, List and Carousel views. START in naming dialogs Accept the file or folder name (Done). R3 on Home (without SELECT) Toggle the background-only display; press R3 again to restore the interface. SELECT + R3 still follows the configured launch key. Triangle / Options Open Options; Settings opens Changes for Save/Discard. One-launch IOP override When starting an ELF application, tap Confirm and release to use the saved Settings > Launching > Reset IOP before launch choice. Hold the configured Confirm button continuously for one second to use its opposite for this launch: On becomes keep IOP, and Off becomes reboot IOP. The hold is detected without a popup. The saved setting is unchanged, including if preparation is cancelled or the launch fails. The choice is captured before application preparation. It also works with launch arguments. Keyboard and automatic launches without a held controller Confirm use the saved setting. Neutrino, POPStarter and Ember game-image launches retain their dedicated device-handoff policies. Visible navigation L1/R1 cycles Home, Files, Tools and Settings. Home contains Applications and Last folder; Files opens directly into a persistent native browser. Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. Automatic-start and launch-key target pickers retain MISC/ . Returning to Files preserves the folder and selection. File Browser Control Action Confirm Open folder/file or launch a supported executable/image. Square Mark/unmark entry. Triangle Options. Back Parent folder; at the Files tab device list, stay on Files. Text Editor See Text Editor navigation . L1/R1 moves to line boundaries with the keyboard hidden; L2/R2 pages vertically. With the virtual keyboard open, the D-pad selects keys and tilting the right stick moves the text cursor left/right and up/down. The compact footer keeps all six hints inside the dashboard safe width."},{"title":"Dashboard","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html#dashboard","text":"Control Action Confirm Open focused item, device or page. Back Close or return. L1 / R1 Previous / next top-level section. SELECT in Home > Applications Cycle Grid, List and Carousel views. START in naming dialogs Accept the file or folder name (Done). R3 on Home (without SELECT) Toggle the background-only display; press R3 again to restore the interface. SELECT + R3 still follows the configured launch key. Triangle / Options Open Options; Settings opens Changes for Save/Discard."},{"title":"One-launch IOP override","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html#elf-iop-override","text":"When starting an ELF application, tap Confirm and release to use the saved Settings > Launching > Reset IOP before launch choice. Hold the configured Confirm button continuously for one second to use its opposite for this launch: On becomes keep IOP, and Off becomes reboot IOP. The hold is detected without a popup. The saved setting is unchanged, including if preparation is cancelled or the launch fails. The choice is captured before application preparation. It also works with launch arguments. Keyboard and automatic launches without a held controller Confirm use the saved setting. Neutrino, POPStarter and Ember game-image launches retain their dedicated device-handoff policies."},{"title":"Visible navigation","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html#tabs","text":"L1/R1 cycles Home, Files, Tools and Settings. Home contains Applications and Last folder; Files opens directly into a persistent native browser. Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. Automatic-start and launch-key target pickers retain MISC/ . Returning to Files preserves the folder and selection."},{"title":"File Browser","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html#browser","text":"Control Action Confirm Open folder/file or launch a supported executable/image. Square Mark/unmark entry. Triangle Options. Back Parent folder; at the Files tab device list, stay on Files."},{"title":"Text Editor","cat":"interface","url":"controls.html#editor","text":"See Text Editor navigation . L1/R1 moves to line boundaries with the keyboard hidden; L2/R2 pages vertically. With the virtual keyboard open, the D-pad selects keys and tilting the right stick moves the text cursor left/right and up/down. The compact footer keeps all six hints inside the dashboard safe width."},{"title":"Device Support","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html","text":"Device Support The dashboard distinguishes Ready , Not checked , Absent and Busy states instead of collapsing them into one disconnected label. Storage matrix Device Browse Demand-load PS2 ISO via Neutrino Memory card Yes No Useful as Neutrino loader location; not normal ISO storage USB / MASS Yes Yes Yes MX4SIO Yes Yes Yes MMCE Yes Yes Yes Internal HDD (APA/PFS) Yes Yes File/app workflows; raw game layouts are backend-specific ATA/exFAT Yes Yes Yes UDPFS Where configured Network stack Yes Why Not checked is selectable A demand-loaded driver cannot know whether hardware is absent until it runs. A Not checked device therefore remains navigable; opening it triggers the existing lazy loader. A confirmed Absent card remains visible with its status and is skipped by focus navigation. USB units USB is labeled USB / MASS . The implementation discovers available units and does not treat mass0: as a permanent identity. MX4SIO and Memory Card Slot 2 MX4SIO owns the second memory-card slot while active. Opening mc1:/ in Files or a file/folder picker first resets the IOP to release MX4SIO, then reads the memory card. The slot stays selectable even if its cached presence was empty. Background card polling and application scans leave MX4SIO running; opening mc0:/ does not require this switch. Switching between MMCE and MX4SIO Opening either mmce0:/ or mmce1:/ while MX4SIO is active resets the IOP before loading MMCE and reading the device. Switching from either MMCE slot to MX4SIO follows the same reset gate. A refused reset stops the switch. Both MMCE slots share one driver and can be browsed in the same IOP session. USB enumeration and intermittent stalls When the USB stack starts, Launchstation probes at most four times with spaced checks, including late second-drive discovery. Each probe uses a synchronous PS2 IOP filesystem RPC: the number of retries is bounded, but a single blocked driver call cannot be timed out safely by the EE. The controller's analog light going out can indicate a stalled PAD/SIO2 or IOP session; it does not, by itself, prove a software reset. An overloaded USB power rail is also possible with two bus-powered drives. Compare behavior with one drive, a powered hub, and the older launcher baseline. Do not hot-unplug a drive during active writes. Tools: USB unit diagnostics Use Tools > USB unit diagnostics to inspect the last cached logical-unit mask without new I/O or explicitly probe USB unit 0 or 1 one at a time. A manual probe is a synchronous IOP filesystem call; a failed driver can block despite bounded retry logic. Logical usb0: / usb1: numbers are not physical port identifiers. Consult the USB stability audit for technical history."},{"title":"Storage matrix","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#matrix","text":"Device Browse Demand-load PS2 ISO via Neutrino Memory card Yes No Useful as Neutrino loader location; not normal ISO storage USB / MASS Yes Yes Yes MX4SIO Yes Yes Yes MMCE Yes Yes Yes Internal HDD (APA/PFS) Yes Yes File/app workflows; raw game layouts are backend-specific ATA/exFAT Yes Yes Yes UDPFS Where configured Network stack Yes"},{"title":"Why Not checked is selectable","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#states","text":"A demand-loaded driver cannot know whether hardware is absent until it runs. A Not checked device therefore remains navigable; opening it triggers the existing lazy loader. A confirmed Absent card remains visible with its status and is skipped by focus navigation."},{"title":"USB units","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#usb","text":"USB is labeled USB / MASS . The implementation discovers available units and does not treat mass0: as a permanent identity."},{"title":"MX4SIO and Memory Card Slot 2","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#mx4sio-mc1","text":"MX4SIO owns the second memory-card slot while active. Opening mc1:/ in Files or a file/folder picker first resets the IOP to release MX4SIO, then reads the memory card. The slot stays selectable even if its cached presence was empty. Background card polling and application scans leave MX4SIO running; opening mc0:/ does not require this switch."},{"title":"Switching between MMCE and MX4SIO","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#mmce-mx4sio","text":"Opening either mmce0:/ or mmce1:/ while MX4SIO is active resets the IOP before loading MMCE and reading the device. Switching from either MMCE slot to MX4SIO follows the same reset gate. A refused reset stops the switch. Both MMCE slots share one driver and can be browsed in the same IOP session."},{"title":"USB enumeration and intermittent stalls","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#usb-stability","text":"When the USB stack starts, Launchstation probes at most four times with spaced checks, including late second-drive discovery. Each probe uses a synchronous PS2 IOP filesystem RPC: the number of retries is bounded, but a single blocked driver call cannot be timed out safely by the EE. The controller's analog light going out can indicate a stalled PAD/SIO2 or IOP session; it does not, by itself, prove a software reset. An overloaded USB power rail is also possible with two bus-powered drives. Compare behavior with one drive, a powered hub, and the older launcher baseline. Do not hot-unplug a drive during active writes."},{"title":"Tools: USB unit diagnostics","cat":"storage & devices","url":"devices.html#usb-diagnostics","text":"Use Tools > USB unit diagnostics to inspect the last cached logical-unit mask without new I/O or explicitly probe USB unit 0 or 1 one at a time. A manual probe is a synchronous IOP filesystem call; a failed driver can block despite bounded retry logic. Logical usb0: / usb1: numbers are not physical port identifiers. Consult the USB stability audit for technical history."},{"title":"Internal HDD","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html","text":"Internal HDD Launchstation retains classic APA/PFS management while surfacing HDD state through the dashboard. APA / PFS Open Files and select the internal HDD from the browser root. Opening an unprobed device starts its lazy HDD stack and exposes the APA/PFS partitions through the existing browser. Not checked remains actionable; confirmed absence does not. HDD Manager Tools → HDD Manager exposes classic partition-management functions such as create, remove, rename, expand, format and HDL-related information. Partition operations can destroy data; make backups before resizing, removing or formatting anything. Path forms Browser paths commonly use hdd0:/partition/path/to/file . Internally a mounted filesystem is represented as pfsN: . Loader helpers retain the owning APA partition so an ELF on PFS remains reachable through teardown/handoff. Common partition workflows __common is frequently used for shared application/data storage. The timestamp organizer also understands hdd0:/__common/ alongside memory-card roots. ATA / exFAT is separate ATA/exFAT storage is not the same thing as APA/PFS. It uses the BDM/FATFS path and appears as its own device class where compiled. Neutrino can use the ATA backend for an ISO on this path."},{"title":"APA / PFS","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html#apa","text":"Open Files and select the internal HDD from the browser root. Opening an unprobed device starts its lazy HDD stack and exposes the APA/PFS partitions through the existing browser. Not checked remains actionable; confirmed absence does not."},{"title":"HDD Manager","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html#manager","text":"Tools → HDD Manager exposes classic partition-management functions such as create, remove, rename, expand, format and HDL-related information. Partition operations can destroy data; make backups before resizing, removing or formatting anything."},{"title":"Path forms","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html#paths","text":"Browser paths commonly use hdd0:/partition/path/to/file . Internally a mounted filesystem is represented as pfsN: . Loader helpers retain the owning APA partition so an ELF on PFS remains reachable through teardown/handoff."},{"title":"Common partition workflows","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html#common","text":"__common is frequently used for shared application/data storage. The timestamp organizer also understands hdd0:/__common/ alongside memory-card roots."},{"title":"ATA / exFAT is separate","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd.html#exfat","text":"ATA/exFAT storage is not the same thing as APA/PFS. It uses the BDM/FATFS path and appears as its own device class where compiled. Neutrino can use the ATA backend for an ISO on this path."},{"title":"HDD Manager & APA Headers","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html","text":"HDD Manager & APA Header Injection Classic APA management is retained, with header injection tools for rebuilding or provisioning HDD-OSD style partition metadata. Partition operations The HDD Manager can inspect APA partitions and exposes creation/removal/rename/expand/format and related maintenance operations where supported. These actions are destructive; confirm the target disk and partition before writing. Browse partitions and game information The partition list displays original APA identifiers, as in classic wLaunchELF, without querying HDL game titles on every cursor movement. Scroll or page using the D-pad and L2/R2. For a selected HDL game partition, press R1 — Game info to load its title and disc information explicitly. The selected partition stays identified by its APA name; long names and startup IDs are fitted inside the details panel. PFS usage can still load in the background. L1 opens HDD status. Inject Header For a selected PFS partition, the operations menu can inject header resources from device:/__Headers/<matching partition name>/ . Supported source families include USB, MMCE and UDPFS. Injection modes Mode Behavior This partition only Inject only the selected partition from its matching header folder. Matching partitions Scan __Headers and inject folders whose partitions already exist. Matching and create missing Inject existing matches, then prompt before creating each missing valid partition. Header files File Status Destination / role Maximum system.cnf Required APA header configuration 512 B icon.sys Required on PS2 Icon metadata 1,024 B list.ico Required on PS2 HDD-OSD icon 1,112,064 B boot.kelf / BOOT.KELF Optional Executable KELF in header 978,944 B BOOT.ELF Optional pfs:/BOOT.ELF , useful with a KELF forwarder Filesystem-dependent info.sys Optional pfs:/res/info.sys Filesystem-dependent jkt_001.png Optional PSBBN jacket image Format-specific jkt_002.png Optional PSX XMB image Format-specific jkt_cp.png Optional PSX copyright image Format-specific Example system.cnf BOOT2 = pfs:/boot.kelf VER = 1.00 VMODE = NTSC HDDUNITPOWER = NICHDD Safety Header injection changes boot-visible partition metadata and can optionally create partitions. Keep known-good header folders and a backup of anything important before bulk operations."},{"title":"Partition operations","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#manager","text":"The HDD Manager can inspect APA partitions and exposes creation/removal/rename/expand/format and related maintenance operations where supported. These actions are destructive; confirm the target disk and partition before writing."},{"title":"Browse partitions and game information","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#browse","text":"The partition list displays original APA identifiers, as in classic wLaunchELF, without querying HDL game titles on every cursor movement. Scroll or page using the D-pad and L2/R2. For a selected HDL game partition, press R1 — Game info to load its title and disc information explicitly. The selected partition stays identified by its APA name; long names and startup IDs are fitted inside the details panel. PFS usage can still load in the background. L1 opens HDD status."},{"title":"Inject Header","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#inject","text":"For a selected PFS partition, the operations menu can inject header resources from device:/__Headers/<matching partition name>/ . Supported source families include USB, MMCE and UDPFS."},{"title":"Injection modes","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#modes","text":"Mode Behavior This partition only Inject only the selected partition from its matching header folder. Matching partitions Scan __Headers and inject folders whose partitions already exist. Matching and create missing Inject existing matches, then prompt before creating each missing valid partition."},{"title":"Header files","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#header-files","text":"File Status Destination / role Maximum system.cnf Required APA header configuration 512 B icon.sys Required on PS2 Icon metadata 1,024 B list.ico Required on PS2 HDD-OSD icon 1,112,064 B boot.kelf / BOOT.KELF Optional Executable KELF in header 978,944 B BOOT.ELF Optional pfs:/BOOT.ELF , useful with a KELF forwarder Filesystem-dependent info.sys Optional pfs:/res/info.sys Filesystem-dependent jkt_001.png Optional PSBBN jacket image Format-specific jkt_002.png Optional PSX XMB image Format-specific jkt_cp.png Optional PSX copyright image Format-specific"},{"title":"Example system.cnf","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#systemcnf","text":"BOOT2 = pfs:/boot.kelf VER = 1.00 VMODE = NTSC HDDUNITPOWER = NICHDD"},{"title":"Safety","cat":"storage & devices","url":"hdd-manager.html#safety","text":"Header injection changes boot-visible partition metadata and can optionally create partitions. Keep known-good header folders and a backup of anything important before bulk operations."},{"title":"Network & PC","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html","text":"Network & PC Integration Network features are demand-loaded where practical so normal file-manager startup does not pay for an unused network stack. FTP Server The R3Z work includes an FTP workflow compatible with its modernized network stack. Start network services intentionally from Tools/settings rather than keeping them resident for every boot. Switching network modes FTP Server, PC Host, UDPFS and Internet clients (including clock sync) use separate IOP sessions. Switching between them automatically reboots the IOP before loading the requested modules. Starting the active mode again reuses it. Switching away stops FTP and invalidates open device handles; direct copies between Host and UDPFS are rejected before opening files. MrRexD-compatible remote commands The project follows the existing R3Z PC-side command ecosystem instead of inventing an unnecessary second desktop protocol. Supported remote commands can request launch/transfer actions using the compatible client. PC game receiver / UDPFS PC game receiver and UDPFS functionality provide network-backed workflows. UDPFS is also an accepted Neutrino ISO backend when the filesystem/server and IP configuration are valid. IP configuration Network settings use the classic IP, netmask and gateway configuration model. The dashboard tracks unsaved network state separately and can write an updated IPCONFIG-style snapshot. Internet clock synchronization Use Settings > Network > Sync PS2 clock from Internet . It loads the Ethernet IP stack and socket RPC server when requested, then queries 0.pool.ntp.org . As in NTPS2, the client records the local timer when the time arrives and adds elapsed time before writing and verifying the PS2 hardware clock. It uses the saved IP, netmask and gateway; the gateway provides DNS with a public resolver as fallback when no resolver is already configured. The reply timeout is five seconds, with one retry for a failed request. A server refusal is not retried. Errors identify driver startup, DNS, socket, timeout or reply-validation failures. This action does not start FTP or netfs. Failure isolation Confirm physical link and adapter initialization. Confirm IP/netmask/gateway. Confirm service/filesystem discovery. Only then debug FTP, UDPFS or another upper-layer feature. Native HTTPS sockets have bounded connect/handshake/read/write waits. DNS resolution in PS2IP remains synchronous and is a separate hardware edge case."},{"title":"FTP Server","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#ftp","text":"The R3Z work includes an FTP workflow compatible with its modernized network stack. Start network services intentionally from Tools/settings rather than keeping them resident for every boot."},{"title":"Switching network modes","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#network-modes","text":"FTP Server, PC Host, UDPFS and Internet clients (including clock sync) use separate IOP sessions. Switching between them automatically reboots the IOP before loading the requested modules. Starting the active mode again reuses it. Switching away stops FTP and invalidates open device handles; direct copies between Host and UDPFS are rejected before opening files."},{"title":"MrRexD-compatible remote commands","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#remote","text":"The project follows the existing R3Z PC-side command ecosystem instead of inventing an unnecessary second desktop protocol. Supported remote commands can request launch/transfer actions using the compatible client."},{"title":"PC game receiver / UDPFS","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#receiver","text":"PC game receiver and UDPFS functionality provide network-backed workflows. UDPFS is also an accepted Neutrino ISO backend when the filesystem/server and IP configuration are valid."},{"title":"IP configuration","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#ip","text":"Network settings use the classic IP, netmask and gateway configuration model. The dashboard tracks unsaved network state separately and can write an updated IPCONFIG-style snapshot."},{"title":"Internet clock synchronization","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#clock-sync","text":"Use Settings > Network > Sync PS2 clock from Internet . It loads the Ethernet IP stack and socket RPC server when requested, then queries 0.pool.ntp.org . As in NTPS2, the client records the local timer when the time arrives and adds elapsed time before writing and verifying the PS2 hardware clock. It uses the saved IP, netmask and gateway; the gateway provides DNS with a public resolver as fallback when no resolver is already configured. The reply timeout is five seconds, with one retry for a failed request. A server refusal is not retried. Errors identify driver startup, DNS, socket, timeout or reply-validation failures. This action does not start FTP or netfs."},{"title":"Failure isolation","cat":"storage & devices","url":"network.html#failure","text":"Confirm physical link and adapter initialization. Confirm IP/netmask/gateway. Confirm service/filesystem discovery. Only then debug FTP, UDPFS or another upper-layer feature. Native HTTPS sockets have bounded connect/handshake/read/write waits. DNS resolution in PS2IP remains synchronous and is a separate hardware edge case."},{"title":"MMCE","cat":"storage & devices","url":"mmce.html","text":"MMCE Browse MMCE SD storage, manage files and launch supported applications and games. Browsing MMCE devices are represented through the device layer and can be opened on demand. The code does not reduce all MMCE use to one hard-coded slot; active device/card state is tracked explicitly. Neutrino Neutrino has a dedicated mmce backend. Launchstation can also discover a complete /neutrino/ install on the game device or on either memory-card slot. Neutrino Game ID VMC selection Launchstation loads MMCE on demand when launching supported Neutrino games and requests the Game ID VMC on responding adapters. MX4SIO exclusions and the emulated memory-card slot holding Neutrino follow R3Z 4.80. See Neutrino Game ID handling ."},{"title":"Browsing","cat":"storage & devices","url":"mmce.html#browse","text":"MMCE devices are represented through the device layer and can be opened on demand. The code does not reduce all MMCE use to one hard-coded slot; active device/card state is tracked explicitly."},{"title":"Neutrino","cat":"storage & devices","url":"mmce.html#neutrino","text":"Neutrino has a dedicated mmce backend. Launchstation can also discover a complete /neutrino/ install on the game device or on either memory-card slot."},{"title":"Neutrino Game ID VMC selection","cat":"storage & devices","url":"mmce.html#game-id","text":"Launchstation loads MMCE on demand when launching supported Neutrino games and requests the Game ID VMC on responding adapters. MX4SIO exclusions and the emulated memory-card slot holding Neutrino follow R3Z 4.80. See Neutrino Game ID handling ."},{"title":"Saves, PSU & Timestamps","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html","text":"Saves, PSU & Timestamp Tools Launchstation keeps the classic memory-card/save toolbox while making destructive actions and timestamp manipulation more explicit. PSU create and extract The browser uses explicit Create PSU and Extract PSU wording so the direction of the conversion is clear. PSU containers are useful for moving save folders while preserving PS2 metadata that ordinary host filesystems do not represent well. Copying saves Copy/paste and PSU workflows retain the specialized handling required by memory-card folders. Avoid treating a PS2 save as an arbitrary PC directory when preserving icon metadata, attributes and timestamps matters. Virtual memory cards VMC image files can be mounted through the File Browser where supported, exposing their contents through the normal file-management path. Timestamp manipulation Launchstation can edit and organize directory timestamps. This is especially useful for OSDSYS/HOSDSYS ordering and icon-based exploit folders whose position is influenced by timestamps. Organizer roots The organizer supports memory-card roots and hdd0:/__common/ . Sorting modes include manual ordering, A–Z and SAS-style timestamps; game-save handling is kept distinct where appropriate. The native updater preserves an existing SAS date on its own app folder after updating the ELF and metadata, so its position does not need to be organized again. Exploit-folder protection Dangerous modifications/deletions of known exploit-style memory-card folders trigger warnings. That guard exists because a routine rename/delete can turn a bootable exploit card into an unbootable one."},{"title":"PSU create and extract","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#psu","text":"The browser uses explicit Create PSU and Extract PSU wording so the direction of the conversion is clear. PSU containers are useful for moving save folders while preserving PS2 metadata that ordinary host filesystems do not represent well."},{"title":"Copying saves","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#copy","text":"Copy/paste and PSU workflows retain the specialized handling required by memory-card folders. Avoid treating a PS2 save as an arbitrary PC directory when preserving icon metadata, attributes and timestamps matters."},{"title":"Virtual memory cards","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#vmc","text":"VMC image files can be mounted through the File Browser where supported, exposing their contents through the normal file-management path."},{"title":"Timestamp manipulation","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#timestamps","text":"Launchstation can edit and organize directory timestamps. This is especially useful for OSDSYS/HOSDSYS ordering and icon-based exploit folders whose position is influenced by timestamps."},{"title":"Organizer roots","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#organizer","text":"The organizer supports memory-card roots and hdd0:/__common/ . Sorting modes include manual ordering, A–Z and SAS-style timestamps; game-save handling is kept distinct where appropriate. The native updater preserves an existing SAS date on its own app folder after updating the ELF and metadata, so its position does not need to be organized again."},{"title":"Exploit-folder protection","cat":"storage & devices","url":"saves.html#protection","text":"Dangerous modifications/deletions of known exploit-style memory-card folders trigger warnings. That guard exists because a routine rename/delete can turn a bootable exploit card into an unbootable one."},{"title":"PS2 ISO · Neutrino","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html","text":"PS2 ISO Launching · Neutrino Open a PS2 .ISO in File Browser and Launchstation hands it to an external Neutrino installation. External core Neutrino is not embedded in Launchstation. Install a compatible Neutrino package separately; Launchstation resolves it, validates the image, builds argv and preserves the backing-store stack for handoff. Loader search order Explicit path — Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders → Neutrino. ISO's device — case-tolerant /NEUTRINO/neutrino.elf and /neutrino/neutrino.elf forms. mc0: — same case-tolerant forms. mc1: — same case-tolerant forms. Usable installation The candidate needs a readable ELF and either modern config/system.toml or legacy system.toml beside the ELF. This follows the current RiptOPL-style completeness rule and avoids requiring one specific Neutrino module-folder generation. Per-launch compatibility and video options Select a valid PS2 ISO to open the R3Z Neutrino options screen. Run game is the first row and is highlighted when the dashboard menu opens. Five independent compatibility choices: fast disc reads (0) , synchronous disc reads (2) , unhook system calls (3) , emulate dual-layer DVD (5) , and fix game buffer overrun (7) . Neutrino mode 1 is a dummy and is intentionally omitted. Video choices: original 480i/576i, auto 240p/288p, auto 480p/576p and 1080i at width x1, x2 or x3. Video compatibility: Off or field-flipping modes 1–3. Debug Colors and PS2 Logo are independent toggles. Use D-pad to select/change, then Run or Cancel. Without a saved profile, options default to Off/Original on every launch. Optional per-game profiles For an ISO with a valid SYSTEM.CNF Game ID such as SLUS_123.45 , the dashboard loads previously saved Neutrino options before showing the menu. Choose Save for this game to persist the current choices, or Reset this launch to clear the current selections without deleting the stored profile. Normal Run does not save changes. Profiles use two checksum-verified NEUT-<GameID>-A.CNF / -B.CNF files in Launchstation's own dashboard settings location, rather than beside the ISO or inside LAUNCHELF.CNF . Each save writes the older/invalid slot; the last valid one remains available after an interrupted write. Only verified Game IDs can be saved, and a malformed profile is ignored. If the settings storage is unavailable or would require an incompatible MMCE/MX4SIO memory-card stack, Launchstation skips the profile without affecting game launch. No active VMC or existing game data is changed. These are Neutrino command-line options , not changes to Launchstation's native disc drivers or global screen calibration. Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders offers independent Disable Ember/POPStarter/Neutrino switches (Off by default). Launch arguments -bsd=<backend> -dvd=<device:path/to/game.iso> -qb -cwd=<neutrino install directory> Current Neutrino documents file-mode paths such as usb: , mx4sio: , mmce: , udpfs: and ata: . The numeric unit suffix, where present, is accepted by Neutrino's backend detector. Supported sources Launchstation source Neutrino BSD USB / MASS usb MX4SIO mx4sio MMCE mmce ATA/exFAT ata UDPFS udpfs Image validation Launchstation opens the selected image and checks the ISO9660 primary volume descriptor at sector 16 for the CD001 signature before handing control to Neutrino. Game ID, RetroGem and MMCE cards Launchstation reads a valid PS2 title ID such as SLUS_123.45 from the selected ISO's root SYSTEM.CNF BOOT2 line. When the Game ID for discs setting is enabled, it displays the RetroGem-compatible launch signal. The reader works independently of MMCE and does not load or reset storage drivers. With MMCE support, Launchstation follows R3Z 4.80 : it pings each adapter, sends the Game ID to responding adapters and waits up to 15 one-second status polls after an accepted request. An ISO or Neutrino ELF stored on mmce0: or mmce1: uses the SD filesystem and does not exclude that adapter from VMC selection. If Neutrino is loaded from the emulated memory-card filesystem mc0: or mc1: , that slot is left unchanged because the loader still needs to read the ELF. The resolved ELF path identifies the slot when an mc: alias was configured. MMCE switching is skipped when the ISO or Neutrino uses MX4SIO, or its driver is still active. Missing Game IDs, absent adapters and rejected commands do not block the game launch. Keep-IOP handoff Quick-boot consumes the already-mounted backing store. ISO launches therefore use a dedicated keep-IOP loader path and do not obey the generic “Reset IOP before ELF launch” preference. If a game does not boot Verify the same ISO can be read in File Browser. Set Neutrino explicitly to remove discovery ambiguity. Confirm its system TOML layout. Try a known-good ISO. Report exact Launchstation hash, Neutrino version and device backend. MMCE game-ID cards After confirming a launch, Launchstation initializes MMCE before preparing Neutrino and requests the ISO's game-ID VMC on responding adapters before handoff. No driver auto-start setting is needed. MMCE is left untouched when the ISO or Neutrino ELF uses MX4SIO. Only an emulated mc0: / mc1: slot holding Neutrino stays unchanged; mmceN: SD paths remain eligible. Missing hardware or a rejected request does not block the game."},{"title":"Loader search order","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#search-order","text":"Explicit path — Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders → Neutrino. ISO's device — case-tolerant /NEUTRINO/neutrino.elf and /neutrino/neutrino.elf forms. mc0: — same case-tolerant forms. mc1: — same case-tolerant forms."},{"title":"Usable installation","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#complete","text":"The candidate needs a readable ELF and either modern config/system.toml or legacy system.toml beside the ELF. This follows the current RiptOPL-style completeness rule and avoids requiring one specific Neutrino module-folder generation."},{"title":"Per-launch compatibility and video options","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#options","text":"Select a valid PS2 ISO to open the R3Z Neutrino options screen. Run game is the first row and is highlighted when the dashboard menu opens. Five independent compatibility choices: fast disc reads (0) , synchronous disc reads (2) , unhook system calls (3) , emulate dual-layer DVD (5) , and fix game buffer overrun (7) . Neutrino mode 1 is a dummy and is intentionally omitted. Video choices: original 480i/576i, auto 240p/288p, auto 480p/576p and 1080i at width x1, x2 or x3. Video compatibility: Off or field-flipping modes 1–3. Debug Colors and PS2 Logo are independent toggles. Use D-pad to select/change, then Run or Cancel. Without a saved profile, options default to Off/Original on every launch."},{"title":"Optional per-game profiles","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#profiles","text":"For an ISO with a valid SYSTEM.CNF Game ID such as SLUS_123.45 , the dashboard loads previously saved Neutrino options before showing the menu. Choose Save for this game to persist the current choices, or Reset this launch to clear the current selections without deleting the stored profile. Normal Run does not save changes. Profiles use two checksum-verified NEUT-<GameID>-A.CNF / -B.CNF files in Launchstation's own dashboard settings location, rather than beside the ISO or inside LAUNCHELF.CNF . Each save writes the older/invalid slot; the last valid one remains available after an interrupted write. Only verified Game IDs can be saved, and a malformed profile is ignored. If the settings storage is unavailable or would require an incompatible MMCE/MX4SIO memory-card stack, Launchstation skips the profile without affecting game launch. No active VMC or existing game data is changed. These are Neutrino command-line options , not changes to Launchstation's native disc drivers or global screen calibration. Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders offers independent Disable Ember/POPStarter/Neutrino switches (Off by default)."},{"title":"Launch arguments","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#arguments","text":"-bsd=<backend> -dvd=<device:path/to/game.iso> -qb -cwd=<neutrino install directory> Current Neutrino documents file-mode paths such as usb: , mx4sio: , mmce: , udpfs: and ata: . The numeric unit suffix, where present, is accepted by Neutrino's backend detector."},{"title":"Supported sources","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#backends","text":"Launchstation source Neutrino BSD USB / MASS usb MX4SIO mx4sio MMCE mmce ATA/exFAT ata UDPFS udpfs"},{"title":"Image validation","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#validation","text":"Launchstation opens the selected image and checks the ISO9660 primary volume descriptor at sector 16 for the CD001 signature before handing control to Neutrino."},{"title":"Game ID, RetroGem and MMCE cards","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#game-id","text":"Launchstation reads a valid PS2 title ID such as SLUS_123.45 from the selected ISO's root SYSTEM.CNF BOOT2 line. When the Game ID for discs setting is enabled, it displays the RetroGem-compatible launch signal. The reader works independently of MMCE and does not load or reset storage drivers. With MMCE support, Launchstation follows R3Z 4.80 : it pings each adapter, sends the Game ID to responding adapters and waits up to 15 one-second status polls after an accepted request. An ISO or Neutrino ELF stored on mmce0: or mmce1: uses the SD filesystem and does not exclude that adapter from VMC selection. If Neutrino is loaded from the emulated memory-card filesystem mc0: or mc1: , that slot is left unchanged because the loader still needs to read the ELF. The resolved ELF path identifies the slot when an mc: alias was configured. MMCE switching is skipped when the ISO or Neutrino uses MX4SIO, or its driver is still active. Missing Game IDs, absent adapters and rejected commands do not block the game launch."},{"title":"Keep-IOP handoff","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#iop","text":"Quick-boot consumes the already-mounted backing store. ISO launches therefore use a dedicated keep-IOP loader path and do not obey the generic “Reset IOP before ELF launch” preference."},{"title":"If a game does not boot","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#troubleshooting","text":"Verify the same ISO can be read in File Browser. Set Neutrino explicitly to remove discovery ambiguity. Confirm its system TOML layout. Try a known-good ISO. Report exact Launchstation hash, Neutrino version and device backend."},{"title":"MMCE game-ID cards","cat":"game images","url":"neutrino.html#mmce-game-card","text":"After confirming a launch, Launchstation initializes MMCE before preparing Neutrino and requests the ISO's game-ID VMC on responding adapters before handoff. No driver auto-start setting is needed. MMCE is left untouched when the ISO or Neutrino ELF uses MX4SIO. Only an emulated mc0: / mc1: slot holding Neutrino stays unchanged; mmceN: SD paths remain eligible. Missing hardware or a rejected request does not block the game."},{"title":"PS1 VCD · POPStarter","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html","text":"PS1 VCD Launching · POPStarter Open a POPStarter-compatible .VCD and Launchstation prepares the external POPStarter handoff. Preferred POPStarter Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders → POPStarter selects the preferred executable. If it cannot be opened, device-specific fallback locations are tried. Common fallback locations mass:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mmce0:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mmce1:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mx4sio:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF ata0:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF hdd0:__common:pfs:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF hdd0:__system:pfs:/launcher/POPSTARTER.ELF Device-aware argument construction Launchstation does not simply launch POPStarter with the browser filename. FAT-style POPS folders, __.POPS , partition-style VCDs and HDD/PFS browser paths are normalized into the argument form POPStarter expects. Validation The VCD path is checked before launch, and the integration contains the existing VCD ISO/PVD and SYSTEM.CNF/GameID parsing helpers used by the POPStarter workflow. External software POPStarter is maintained separately and is not distributed as part of Launchstation. Its own compatibility requirements still apply."},{"title":"Preferred POPStarter","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html#setting","text":"Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders → POPStarter selects the preferred executable. If it cannot be opened, device-specific fallback locations are tried."},{"title":"Common fallback locations","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html#fallbacks","text":"mass:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mmce0:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mmce1:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF mx4sio:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF ata0:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF hdd0:__common:pfs:/POPS/POPSTARTER.ELF hdd0:__system:pfs:/launcher/POPSTARTER.ELF"},{"title":"Device-aware argument construction","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html#arguments","text":"Launchstation does not simply launch POPStarter with the browser filename. FAT-style POPS folders, __.POPS , partition-style VCDs and HDD/PFS browser paths are normalized into the argument form POPStarter expects."},{"title":"Validation","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html#validation","text":"The VCD path is checked before launch, and the integration contains the existing VCD ISO/PVD and SYSTEM.CNF/GameID parsing helpers used by the POPStarter workflow."},{"title":"External software","cat":"game images","url":"popstarter.html#external","text":"POPStarter is maintained separately and is not distributed as part of Launchstation. Its own compatibility requirements still apply."},{"title":"PS1 Images · Ember","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html","text":"PS1 Image Launching · Ember Ember is the second PS1 core beside POPStarter. The two do not compete for a preference: .VCD uses POPStarter, while Ember's own folder/image layout uses Ember automatically. External core and BIOS Ember is not embedded in Launchstation. Set the optional Ember path under Settings → Launching → Disc & image loaders by selecting an ELF file. The target game's own umbrella still needs the user's PlayStation BIOS as bios.bin (exactly 512 KB). If the target umbrella lacks ember.elf , Launchstation can copy the selected ELF into that umbrella as ember.elf once before launch. Older folder-valued settings remain supported. Save-safe layout The umbrella folder can be named anything and live anywhere on the device. DEVICE:/ └── MY-EMBER/ ├── ember.elf ├── bios.bin └── games/ ├── Spyro 2 (Ripto's Rage)/ │ ├── game.cue │ ├── track01.bin │ ├── MC1.vmc created/used by Ember │ └── MC2.vmc created/used by Ember └── Another Game/ └── game.bin Select a .CUE , .BIN or PS1 .EXE directly inside the game folder. Launchstation derives the bare folder name and passes that to Ember. Why loose CUE files are refused Ember can resolve looser layouts, but its memory-card path is always composed as games/<argument>/MC1.vmc and MC2.vmc . A loose games/Game.cue can boot while silently making the save path impossible. The browser therefore offers only the folder layout that can both launch and save correctly. Launch contract Ember derives its working directory from target argv[0] , so Launchstation preserves the real live path to ember.elf as target argv[0] . The bare game-folder name is the only game argument. Names containing path separators, a colon, a leading .. , or more than 180 characters are refused. USB reset and final keep-IOP handoff Ember requires a working game-device stack when control reaches it. For USB/MASS, LAUNCHSTATION first finishes background I/O and audio, prepares a clean IOP without its own PADMAN, re-discovers the USB game and ember.elf even if the drive's logical unit has changed, then executes Ember with no further IOP reset . A mismatched or ambiguous remount is refused rather than silently launching another drive. For non-USB paths the existing mounted stack is retained for handoff. On APA/PFS, the Ember partition is mounted writable with write-through for its save files. RetroGEM Game ID Ember CUE/BIN launches use the same brief Game ID signal as POPStarter and Neutrino. Enable Settings > Launching > Game ID output > Game ID launch signal for discs . Launchstation reads the CUE data track's SYSTEM.CNF boot serial, then tries the existing PS1 volume-timestamp database and filename fallback. CUE track offsets are respected; BIN files use a matching CUE when available, or probe supported raw/cooked sector layouts. A standalone PS1 EXE has no disc filesystem to identify. The ID is read before cleanup and displayed for two frames after a successful USB remount, immediately before Ember starts. An unreadable, malformed or unsupported image omits the optional signal and retains the normal launch behavior. This uses the upstream identity implementation and adds no MMCE card-selection command. RetroGEM recognition on physical hardware remains to be verified. POPStarter and Ember together Format/layout Launcher POPS/*.VCD POPStarter MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.CUE Ember MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.BIN Ember MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.EXE Ember Troubleshooting Confirm the selected image is directly under <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ . Confirm <umbrella>/bios.bin exists and is exactly 512 KB. If <umbrella>/ember.elf is missing, choose a valid ELF under Ember path so Launchstation can stage the executable into the target umbrella. Use the dedicated Ember handoff; its controlled USB-only remount and final keep-IOP execution ignore the normal ELF reset preference."},{"title":"Save-safe layout","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#layout","text":"The umbrella folder can be named anything and live anywhere on the device. DEVICE:/ └── MY-EMBER/ ├── ember.elf ├── bios.bin └── games/ ├── Spyro 2 (Ripto's Rage)/ │ ├── game.cue │ ├── track01.bin │ ├── MC1.vmc created/used by Ember │ └── MC2.vmc created/used by Ember └── Another Game/ └── game.bin Select a .CUE , .BIN or PS1 .EXE directly inside the game folder. Launchstation derives the bare folder name and passes that to Ember."},{"title":"Why loose CUE files are refused","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#why-layout","text":"Ember can resolve looser layouts, but its memory-card path is always composed as games/<argument>/MC1.vmc and MC2.vmc . A loose games/Game.cue can boot while silently making the save path impossible. The browser therefore offers only the folder layout that can both launch and save correctly."},{"title":"Launch contract","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#argv","text":"Ember derives its working directory from target argv[0] , so Launchstation preserves the real live path to ember.elf as target argv[0] . The bare game-folder name is the only game argument. Names containing path separators, a colon, a leading .. , or more than 180 characters are refused."},{"title":"USB reset and final keep-IOP handoff","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#iop","text":"Ember requires a working game-device stack when control reaches it. For USB/MASS, LAUNCHSTATION first finishes background I/O and audio, prepares a clean IOP without its own PADMAN, re-discovers the USB game and ember.elf even if the drive's logical unit has changed, then executes Ember with no further IOP reset . A mismatched or ambiguous remount is refused rather than silently launching another drive. For non-USB paths the existing mounted stack is retained for handoff. On APA/PFS, the Ember partition is mounted writable with write-through for its save files."},{"title":"RetroGEM Game ID","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#gameid","text":"Ember CUE/BIN launches use the same brief Game ID signal as POPStarter and Neutrino. Enable Settings > Launching > Game ID output > Game ID launch signal for discs . Launchstation reads the CUE data track's SYSTEM.CNF boot serial, then tries the existing PS1 volume-timestamp database and filename fallback. CUE track offsets are respected; BIN files use a matching CUE when available, or probe supported raw/cooked sector layouts. A standalone PS1 EXE has no disc filesystem to identify. The ID is read before cleanup and displayed for two frames after a successful USB remount, immediately before Ember starts. An unreadable, malformed or unsupported image omits the optional signal and retains the normal launch behavior. This uses the upstream identity implementation and adds no MMCE card-selection command. RetroGEM recognition on physical hardware remains to be verified."},{"title":"POPStarter and Ember together","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#cores","text":"Format/layout Launcher POPS/*.VCD POPStarter MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.CUE Ember MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.BIN Ember MY-EMBER/games/<Game>/*.EXE Ember"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","cat":"game images","url":"ember.html#troubleshooting","text":"Confirm the selected image is directly under <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ . Confirm <umbrella>/bios.bin exists and is exactly 512 KB. If <umbrella>/ember.elf is missing, choose a valid ELF under Ember path so Launchstation can stage the executable into the target umbrella. Use the dedicated Ember handoff; its controlled USB-only remount and final keep-IOP execution ignore the normal ELF reset preference."},{"title":"MC Exploit Installer","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html","text":"Memory Card Exploit Installer The LoadBOOTer-based installer can provision supported PS2/PSX/DEX exploit/update targets and can bind a user-provided ELF when the required signing material is supplied. Installer flow Open the exploit installer from Tools/MISC, choose a supported installation mode, then follow the target-specific prompts. The installer detects console families so it can select the appropriate signing/header behavior. Bind a custom ELF Choose Choose other ELF and select a native ELF. A user-provided PS2KEYS.dat must be in the same source folder: usb0:/APPS/ ├── MyApp.ELF └── PS2KEYS.dat PS2KEYS.dat is intentionally not bundled with this repository and remains at the source location; it is not copied onto the memory card. Console-family selection Detected family Key section Header style Retail SCPH retail DNASLOAD DESR / PSX retail FMCB-style target DTL development systems dev DNASLOAD Arcade / System 246/256 / Python 1 arcade DONGLE Python 2 / SCPH-50000MB family retail DONGLE Signing material The repository documentation may describe the expected PS2KEYS.dat field format or fingerprints, but does not distribute private/proprietary key material. Users are responsible for supplying lawful signing data. Back up the card Exploit installation modifies boot-sensitive files and folders. Make a card backup before replacing an existing exploit or experimenting with a custom payload."},{"title":"Installer flow","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html#builtin","text":"Open the exploit installer from Tools/MISC, choose a supported installation mode, then follow the target-specific prompts. The installer detects console families so it can select the appropriate signing/header behavior."},{"title":"Bind a custom ELF","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html#custom","text":"Choose Choose other ELF and select a native ELF. A user-provided PS2KEYS.dat must be in the same source folder: usb0:/APPS/ ├── MyApp.ELF └── PS2KEYS.dat PS2KEYS.dat is intentionally not bundled with this repository and remains at the source location; it is not copied onto the memory card."},{"title":"Console-family selection","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html#models","text":"Detected family Key section Header style Retail SCPH retail DNASLOAD DESR / PSX retail FMCB-style target DTL development systems dev DNASLOAD Arcade / System 246/256 / Python 1 arcade DONGLE Python 2 / SCPH-50000MB family retail DONGLE"},{"title":"Signing material","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html#keys","text":"The repository documentation may describe the expected PS2KEYS.dat field format or fingerprints, but does not distribute private/proprietary key material. Users are responsible for supplying lawful signing data."},{"title":"Back up the card","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"exploit-installer.html#backup","text":"Exploit installation modifies boot-sensitive files and folders. Make a card backup before replacing an existing exploit or experimenting with a custom payload."},{"title":"FTP, PS2Link & Remote","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html","text":"FTP, PS2Link & Remote Control Network services are included in the standard build but remain demand-driven so normal boots are not slowed by an always-on network stack. FTP Server Start Tools > FTP Server when you need file transfer. FTP Server, PC Host, UDPFS and Internet clock sync use separate network modes. Switching modes automatically reboots the IOP and stops the previous service; selecting the active mode again keeps it running. PS2Link PS2Link support remains available for development-oriented transfer/launch workflows. Like FTP, it is not started during an ordinary dashboard boot. R3Z remote-command compatibility The project follows the existing oMrRexD R3Z/PC-client command ecosystem where possible rather than creating a second incompatible desktop client for the same operations. Recommended troubleshooting sequence Confirm physical Ethernet/link and adapter availability. Confirm IP, netmask and gateway. Start only the intended service. Test basic connectivity from the PC. Then debug the higher-level command/transfer request. Network project credits PS2Link is a ps2dev project. The retained legacy ps2ftpd sources credit Jesper Svennevid and carry Academic Free License version 2.0. The Credits & Licenses page lists their respective roles. Network exposure These are homebrew console services, not hardened internet-facing daemons. Keep the PS2 on a trusted local network and do not forward service ports from the public internet."},{"title":"FTP Server","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#ftp","text":"Start Tools > FTP Server when you need file transfer. FTP Server, PC Host, UDPFS and Internet clock sync use separate network modes. Switching modes automatically reboots the IOP and stops the previous service; selecting the active mode again keeps it running."},{"title":"PS2Link","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#ps2link","text":"PS2Link support remains available for development-oriented transfer/launch workflows. Like FTP, it is not started during an ordinary dashboard boot."},{"title":"R3Z remote-command compatibility","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#remote","text":"The project follows the existing oMrRexD R3Z/PC-client command ecosystem where possible rather than creating a second incompatible desktop client for the same operations."},{"title":"Recommended troubleshooting sequence","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#sequence","text":"Confirm physical Ethernet/link and adapter availability. Confirm IP, netmask and gateway. Start only the intended service. Test basic connectivity from the PC. Then debug the higher-level command/transfer request."},{"title":"Network project credits","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#upstream-credits","text":"PS2Link is a ps2dev project. The retained legacy ps2ftpd sources credit Jesper Svennevid and carry Academic Free License version 2.0. The Credits & Licenses page lists their respective roles."},{"title":"Network exposure","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"network-services.html#security","text":"These are homebrew console services, not hardened internet-facing daemons. Keep the PS2 on a trusted local network and do not forward service ports from the public internet."},{"title":"PSX, Deckard & RetroGem","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html","text":"PSX, Deckard & RetroGem R3Z carries hardware-specific behavior for later slim consoles, DESR PSX systems and RetroGem-equipped consoles. Deckard PS2 models On Deckard-family slim systems without a conventional internal expansion-bay HDD path, internal HDD/ATA cards are hidden where they are not meaningful. This avoids presenting dead hardware paths as normal choices. Sony PSX / DESR PSX-specific devices such as xfrom: and dvr_hdd0: are exposed only on compatible DESR hardware. The exploit installer also recognizes PSX-family targets and chooses the appropriate installation/header behavior. RetroGem Game ID The project includes RetroGem Game ID support for PixelFX RetroGem workflows. This allows compatible launch paths to communicate game identity to the video hardware where configured. Dual HDD / ATA groundwork The standard build includes dual HDD/ATA support groundwork for ongoing development. Treat experimental combinations as hardware-test territory and report exact console/device details when something differs from the primary paths. Hardware-specific reports Always include the exact console model. Behavior that is correct on a fat SCPH with DEV9/HDD can be intentionally different on a Deckard slim or a DESR PSX."},{"title":"Deckard PS2 models","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html#deckard","text":"On Deckard-family slim systems without a conventional internal expansion-bay HDD path, internal HDD/ATA cards are hidden where they are not meaningful. This avoids presenting dead hardware paths as normal choices."},{"title":"Sony PSX / DESR","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html#psx","text":"PSX-specific devices such as xfrom: and dvr_hdd0: are exposed only on compatible DESR hardware. The exploit installer also recognizes PSX-family targets and chooses the appropriate installation/header behavior."},{"title":"RetroGem Game ID","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html#retrogem","text":"The project includes RetroGem Game ID support for PixelFX RetroGem workflows. This allows compatible launch paths to communicate game identity to the video hardware where configured."},{"title":"Dual HDD / ATA groundwork","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html#dual-hdd","text":"The standard build includes dual HDD/ATA support groundwork for ongoing development. Treat experimental combinations as hardware-test territory and report exact console/device details when something differs from the primary paths."},{"title":"Hardware-specific reports","cat":"utilities & hardware","url":"special-hardware.html#reports","text":"Always include the exact console model. Behavior that is correct on a fat SCPH with DEV9/HDD can be intentionally different on a Deckard slim or a DESR PSX."},{"title":"Settings Reference","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html","text":"Settings Reference Dashboard settings are grouped by purpose and retain explicit unsaved-state behavior. Major groups Group Examples Appearance Theme, selection style, icon backing, clock/date, screen position. Home & applications Application folders, startup section/view, Home shortcuts. Sound Dashboard audio and BGM behavior. Input Confirm/cancel mapping, USB keyboard, virtual keyboard layout. Launching Normal ELF IOP reset, ESR, OSDSYS, POPStarter, Ember/PS1 behavior, Neutrino and launch keys. Storage & devices Memory-card/HDD visibility, MMCE history and storage behavior. Network IP, netmask, gateway and network-oriented feature controls. System Core runtime options and maintenance actions. PS2 hardware clock Use Settings > System > Set PS2 clock manually... and enter YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS . Launchstation validates the date, writes the PS2 RTC and verifies the readback. The displayed date and time refresh immediately after a successful write. Network builds also offer Settings > Network > Sync PS2 clock from Internet . With a dead clock battery, the time may need setting again after power loss. PS2 temperature Settings > System > PS2 Temperature Units (50K and later) selects Celsius or Fahrenheit. On supported consoles, temperature appears two spaces after the time, with a degree symbol. The sensor is read every five seconds using the R3Z MECHACON command; if the first probe is unsupported, the temperature is hidden. Unit changes convert the cached sensor reading immediately. The choice is saved in DASHBOARD.CNF. Colored Game ID launch bars Game ID color output for ELF/application launches is off by default on new configurations. It remains available in Settings > Launching > Game ID output for RetroGEM-compatible display adapters. Previously saved configurations retain their selected value until changed. Theme-matched controller button icons Settings > Appearance > Control icon backings controls whether face-button and D-pad symbols receive a theme-derived badge. When disabled, the symbols render without the backing. Button symbols are generated from the built-in vector atlas and need no external icon PNGs. Unsaved state Settings compare current serialized values with the session snapshot. Touching a control and returning it to the original value does not leave a false dirty flag. Save failures remain visible and can be retried. Path pickers SELECT chooses the current folder in folder pickers. Program/file settings use the native browser picker. POPStarter path , Ember path and Neutrino path each select an ELF file. Their popups offer Choose a location... , Clear location and Disabled . Disabled keeps the saved path and prevents that loader from launching. Choosing or clearing a location enables it again; clearing restores the normal discovery rules. Each settings row shows its path, Not set or Disabled. Ember uses the selected executable to supply ember.elf when the game's own umbrella lacks it. The game device still owns bios.bin and games/<Game>/ . Older Ember folder settings remain supported. VCD remains POPStarter; CUE/BIN/EXE remains Ember. Language preview In System > Language , highlighting a built-in language applies it immediately to file-operation and tool messages. The chooser shows translated Copy, Paste and Cancel samples. Confirm keeps the choice for this settings session; Cancel restores the previous language. A configured external .LNG file is reapplied after either action. Save Settings to keep an accepted change. Reset actions Display/theme reset actions restore known project defaults so users do not need to manually reverse each visual setting. Reset IOP for one launch Reset IOP before launch remains the saved default for ordinary ELF launches. Hold Confirm for one second when starting an application to reverse that choice for that launch only; release sooner to use the default. The hold is detected without a popup, and the launch context carries the temporary choice without changing configuration. See One-launch IOP override . Native update install Update checks run network requests on the serialized IO worker, keeping the popup and spinner animated. Status messages show certificate loading, server lookup and connection, secure-connection verification, request transmission and response reception. The comparison phase has its own title. The install confirmation and up-to-date results show the matching standard or DS34 package's last update time as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC . This uses that package's updated_at , since a rolling release retains its original publication date. A missing or invalid timestamp shows Unavailable. Tools > System > Check for updates (below About Launchstation) validates ancestry against the rolling latest release. The updater selects the standard or DS34 PSU package for the running variant, verifies its advertised SHA-256 and contents over trusted HTTPS, stages the verified ELF and title.cfg , then replaces both with matching .BAK recovery copies when originals exist. Metadata comes from the same verified package; its boot entry retains the installed ELF filename. A failed replacement attempts to restore both originals. If the app folder already has its SAS ordering timestamp on a memory card or under hdd0:/__common/ , the updater reapplies that date after all writes and cleanup, including failed updates. Ordinary folder dates are left to the filesystem; a failure to restore SAS is reported. The verified four-file PSU can restore missing updater CA and BGM sidecars, but it does not overwrite existing customized sidecars. Interrupted .UPDATE / .PACKAGE staging files are preserved for manual recovery rather than silently truncated. Recovery-copy failures show the source ELF path and filesystem error code. If a failed backup rename leaves the boot file unreadable, the updater preserves the .BAK and verified .UPDATE files for recovery. Restart LAUNCHSTATION to execute the new build. Older update clients may require a one-time manual package install. Current Home, Apps and Files placement Back moves up settings pages and folders; the Settings root and Files device list no longer have a Home button. Use L1/R1 to change sections. Returning to Settings restores its page, selected row, scroll position and Back history, including after saving or discarding changes. Home includes the Applications library and Last folder shortcut. In Home & applications > Start screen , choose Home, Applications, Files, Tools or Settings. Applications starts directly in Home's library viewer with All applications selected. Home also opens that viewer automatically when Recent applications is 0 and its saved history is empty. Files runs the native File Browser directly; separate Apps and Devices labels are retained only where the old internal section identifiers require compatibility. Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. The popup file browser for automatic-start and launch-key targets retains MISC/ . Video mode confirmation The mode chooser shows each option's nominal signal resolution, progressive ( p ) or interlaced ( i ) scan, and refresh rate on the right. NTSC shows 720x480i 60Hz ; PAL shows 720x576i 50Hz ; VGA shows 640x480p 60Hz ; 480p shows 720x480p 60Hz ; and 576p shows 720x576p 50Hz . Auto shows the console-region output, independently of the currently selected forced mode. The 60Hz labels use the nominal NTSC/480p rate. Unsupported 576p retains its resolution details and an unsupported label. The six choices are Auto, NTSC, PAL, VGA, 480p and 576p. The duplicate 576i entry is removed; older saved 576i settings load as PAL, preserving the same output. A changed video mode is previewed for 10 seconds , giving the display time to resync. Press the shown Confirm button to keep the mode for this settings session, or the Back button to revert. These are direct button actions; D-pad input does not select an action. No confirmation within the countdown also restores the previous mode and screen offsets. Save remains a separate action. The switch waits for outgoing graphics transfers and rendering to finish before resetting the GS, then refreshes the dashboard texture pool. NTSC, VGA and 480p use a 640×448 framebuffer; PAL and 576p use 640×512. The dashboard maps the same 640×448 layout to each framebuffer so panels, text, clipping and spacing keep their relative positions. To inspect a mode, press Confirm to keep it, browse Home, Files, Tools, Settings and the Text Editor, then return to Screen calibration to adjust position or select another mode. Progressive and VGA modes require a compatible display and connection. PAL selects interlaced 576i at 50Hz; 576p selects progressive 576p at 50Hz. Native 576p requires retail PS2 ROM 2.20 or newer, or a detected PSX/DESR console with ROM 1.80J or 2.10J. Unsupported 576p is grayed out in the chooser and skipped with Left/Right. PAL stays available at 50 Hz. If unsupported 576p or an invalid mode was saved, startup changes the selection to Auto before GS initialization and uses the console region. Both outputs use the same 10-second confirmation and rollback. PAL and 576p each reserve 2.5 MiB for two framebuffers, leaving 1.5 MiB for textures; oversized padded textures and palettes are rejected before allocation to keep the interface responsive. Storage driver loading Drivers load when a browser, application or tool needs the device. Per-device driver auto-start settings have been removed. Old Dash_DevAuto and Dash_ShowUnavailableDevices values are ignored and omitted on save. Eligible device families remain listed, and opening an unprobed device starts its existing lazy driver stack. ELF device paths Ordinary ELF launches use the fixed R3Z 4.80 policy: usb: and mass: become mass0:/... , and numbered USB/MASS paths become massN:/... . ATA and MX4SIO keep their device paths. The handoff-path selector has been removed; old Modern_Device_Handoff values are ignored and omitted on save. Automatic start and Start screen Start screen chooses the first view. A configured Settings > Launching > Automatic start & boot > Automatic start target runs afterward only when Automatic start timeout is greater than zero. A timeout of 0s disables automatic start while keeping the target saved, so the selected screen stays open. Held-button launch keys still work at 0s. Any button cancels a running countdown. Save Settings to keep your choice. Startup application discovery The cached application list in DASHLIB.CNF is loaded before the selected Start screen opens. When a library scan runs, it checks memory cards afresh even if an earlier status check reported them absent, and reads other devices whose drivers are already running. A failed card check preserves its cached applications for a later retry. The viewer shows scanning status until the result has been applied. Opening another device, launching an application, or using a storage tool loads the required driver on demand. Background scans skip unloaded stacks."},{"title":"Major groups","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#groups","text":"Group Examples Appearance Theme, selection style, icon backing, clock/date, screen position. Home & applications Application folders, startup section/view, Home shortcuts. Sound Dashboard audio and BGM behavior. Input Confirm/cancel mapping, USB keyboard, virtual keyboard layout. Launching Normal ELF IOP reset, ESR, OSDSYS, POPStarter, Ember/PS1 behavior, Neutrino and launch keys. Storage & devices Memory-card/HDD visibility, MMCE history and storage behavior. Network IP, netmask, gateway and network-oriented feature controls. System Core runtime options and maintenance actions."},{"title":"PS2 hardware clock","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#clock-manual","text":"Use Settings > System > Set PS2 clock manually... and enter YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS . Launchstation validates the date, writes the PS2 RTC and verifies the readback. The displayed date and time refresh immediately after a successful write. Network builds also offer Settings > Network > Sync PS2 clock from Internet . With a dead clock battery, the time may need setting again after power loss."},{"title":"PS2 temperature","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#temperature","text":"Settings > System > PS2 Temperature Units (50K and later) selects Celsius or Fahrenheit. On supported consoles, temperature appears two spaces after the time, with a degree symbol. The sensor is read every five seconds using the R3Z MECHACON command; if the first probe is unsupported, the temperature is hidden. Unit changes convert the cached sensor reading immediately. The choice is saved in DASHBOARD.CNF."},{"title":"Colored Game ID launch bars","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#gameid-launch","text":"Game ID color output for ELF/application launches is off by default on new configurations. It remains available in Settings > Launching > Game ID output for RetroGEM-compatible display adapters. Previously saved configurations retain their selected value until changed."},{"title":"Theme-matched controller button icons","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#control-icon-backing","text":"Settings > Appearance > Control icon backings controls whether face-button and D-pad symbols receive a theme-derived badge. When disabled, the symbols render without the backing. Button symbols are generated from the built-in vector atlas and need no external icon PNGs."},{"title":"Unsaved state","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#dirty","text":"Settings compare current serialized values with the session snapshot. Touching a control and returning it to the original value does not leave a false dirty flag. Save failures remain visible and can be retried."},{"title":"Path pickers","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#paths","text":"SELECT chooses the current folder in folder pickers. Program/file settings use the native browser picker. POPStarter path , Ember path and Neutrino path each select an ELF file. Their popups offer Choose a location... , Clear location and Disabled . Disabled keeps the saved path and prevents that loader from launching. Choosing or clearing a location enables it again; clearing restores the normal discovery rules. Each settings row shows its path, Not set or Disabled. Ember uses the selected executable to supply ember.elf when the game's own umbrella lacks it. The game device still owns bios.bin and games/<Game>/ . Older Ember folder settings remain supported. VCD remains POPStarter; CUE/BIN/EXE remains Ember."},{"title":"Language preview","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#language-preview","text":"In System > Language , highlighting a built-in language applies it immediately to file-operation and tool messages. The chooser shows translated Copy, Paste and Cancel samples. Confirm keeps the choice for this settings session; Cancel restores the previous language. A configured external .LNG file is reapplied after either action. Save Settings to keep an accepted change."},{"title":"Reset actions","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#reset","text":"Display/theme reset actions restore known project defaults so users do not need to manually reverse each visual setting."},{"title":"Reset IOP for one launch","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#elf-iop-override","text":"Reset IOP before launch remains the saved default for ordinary ELF launches. Hold Confirm for one second when starting an application to reverse that choice for that launch only; release sooner to use the default. The hold is detected without a popup, and the launch context carries the temporary choice without changing configuration. See One-launch IOP override ."},{"title":"Native update install","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#native-update","text":"Update checks run network requests on the serialized IO worker, keeping the popup and spinner animated. Status messages show certificate loading, server lookup and connection, secure-connection verification, request transmission and response reception. The comparison phase has its own title. The install confirmation and up-to-date results show the matching standard or DS34 package's last update time as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC . This uses that package's updated_at , since a rolling release retains its original publication date. A missing or invalid timestamp shows Unavailable. Tools > System > Check for updates (below About Launchstation) validates ancestry against the rolling latest release. The updater selects the standard or DS34 PSU package for the running variant, verifies its advertised SHA-256 and contents over trusted HTTPS, stages the verified ELF and title.cfg , then replaces both with matching .BAK recovery copies when originals exist. Metadata comes from the same verified package; its boot entry retains the installed ELF filename. A failed replacement attempts to restore both originals. If the app folder already has its SAS ordering timestamp on a memory card or under hdd0:/__common/ , the updater reapplies that date after all writes and cleanup, including failed updates. Ordinary folder dates are left to the filesystem; a failure to restore SAS is reported. The verified four-file PSU can restore missing updater CA and BGM sidecars, but it does not overwrite existing customized sidecars. Interrupted .UPDATE / .PACKAGE staging files are preserved for manual recovery rather than silently truncated. Recovery-copy failures show the source ELF path and filesystem error code. If a failed backup rename leaves the boot file unreadable, the updater preserves the .BAK and verified .UPDATE files for recovery. Restart LAUNCHSTATION to execute the new build. Older update clients may require a one-time manual package install."},{"title":"Current Home, Apps and Files placement","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#home-navigation","text":"Back moves up settings pages and folders; the Settings root and Files device list no longer have a Home button. Use L1/R1 to change sections. Returning to Settings restores its page, selected row, scroll position and Back history, including after saving or discarding changes. Home includes the Applications library and Last folder shortcut. In Home & applications > Start screen , choose Home, Applications, Files, Tools or Settings. Applications starts directly in Home's library viewer with All applications selected. Home also opens that viewer automatically when Recent applications is 0 and its saved history is empty. Files runs the native File Browser directly; separate Apps and Devices labels are retained only where the old internal section identifiers require compatibility. Tools exposes individual built-in utilities. The popup file browser for automatic-start and launch-key targets retains MISC/ ."},{"title":"Video mode confirmation","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#video-preview","text":"The mode chooser shows each option's nominal signal resolution, progressive ( p ) or interlaced ( i ) scan, and refresh rate on the right. NTSC shows 720x480i 60Hz ; PAL shows 720x576i 50Hz ; VGA shows 640x480p 60Hz ; 480p shows 720x480p 60Hz ; and 576p shows 720x576p 50Hz . Auto shows the console-region output, independently of the currently selected forced mode. The 60Hz labels use the nominal NTSC/480p rate. Unsupported 576p retains its resolution details and an unsupported label. The six choices are Auto, NTSC, PAL, VGA, 480p and 576p. The duplicate 576i entry is removed; older saved 576i settings load as PAL, preserving the same output. A changed video mode is previewed for 10 seconds , giving the display time to resync. Press the shown Confirm button to keep the mode for this settings session, or the Back button to revert. These are direct button actions; D-pad input does not select an action. No confirmation within the countdown also restores the previous mode and screen offsets. Save remains a separate action. The switch waits for outgoing graphics transfers and rendering to finish before resetting the GS, then refreshes the dashboard texture pool. NTSC, VGA and 480p use a 640×448 framebuffer; PAL and 576p use 640×512. The dashboard maps the same 640×448 layout to each framebuffer so panels, text, clipping and spacing keep their relative positions. To inspect a mode, press Confirm to keep it, browse Home, Files, Tools, Settings and the Text Editor, then return to Screen calibration to adjust position or select another mode. Progressive and VGA modes require a compatible display and connection. PAL selects interlaced 576i at 50Hz; 576p selects progressive 576p at 50Hz. Native 576p requires retail PS2 ROM 2.20 or newer, or a detected PSX/DESR console with ROM 1.80J or 2.10J. Unsupported 576p is grayed out in the chooser and skipped with Left/Right. PAL stays available at 50 Hz. If unsupported 576p or an invalid mode was saved, startup changes the selection to Auto before GS initialization and uses the console region. Both outputs use the same 10-second confirmation and rollback. PAL and 576p each reserve 2.5 MiB for two framebuffers, leaving 1.5 MiB for textures; oversized padded textures and palettes are rejected before allocation to keep the interface responsive."},{"title":"Storage driver loading","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#driver-loading","text":"Drivers load when a browser, application or tool needs the device. Per-device driver auto-start settings have been removed. Old Dash_DevAuto and Dash_ShowUnavailableDevices values are ignored and omitted on save. Eligible device families remain listed, and opening an unprobed device starts its existing lazy driver stack."},{"title":"ELF device paths","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#elf-handoff","text":"Ordinary ELF launches use the fixed R3Z 4.80 policy: usb: and mass: become mass0:/... , and numbered USB/MASS paths become massN:/... . ATA and MX4SIO keep their device paths. The handoff-path selector has been removed; old Modern_Device_Handoff values are ignored and omitted on save."},{"title":"Automatic start and Start screen","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#automatic-start","text":"Start screen chooses the first view. A configured Settings > Launching > Automatic start & boot > Automatic start target runs afterward only when Automatic start timeout is greater than zero. A timeout of 0s disables automatic start while keeping the target saved, so the selected screen stays open. Held-button launch keys still work at 0s. Any button cancels a running countdown. Save Settings to keep your choice."},{"title":"Startup application discovery","cat":"customize","url":"settings.html#startup-discovery","text":"The cached application list in DASHLIB.CNF is loaded before the selected Start screen opens. When a library scan runs, it checks memory cards afresh even if an earlier status check reported them absent, and reads other devices whose drivers are already running. A failed card check preserves its cached applications for a later retry. The viewer shows scanning status until the result has been applied. Opening another device, launching an application, or using a storage tool loads the required driver on demand. Background scans skip unloaded stacks."},{"title":"Config Files & Paths","cat":"customize","url":"configuration.html","text":"Configuration Files & Paths Classic CNF compatibility is retained while dashboard features add their own state. LAUNCHELF.CNF Important modern keys include: Key Purpose POPSTARTER_ELF Preferred POPStarter executable for VCD images. EMBER_DIR Ember ELF path; the historical key name is retained. Older folder values still resolve to their ember.elf. The executable can be staged into a game device's umbrella when needed. NEUTRINO_ELF Preferred Neutrino executable. REBOOT_IOP_ELFLOAD Generic normal-ELF reset behavior. Neutrino ISO handoff deliberately does not use it. CNF_Path Configuration load/save override. USBKBD_FILE / KBDMAP_FILE USB keyboard driver/map paths. Save locations The dashboard can save classic configuration to the configured folder, next to the running ELF or a memory-card SYS-CONF target. The path used to load configuration is tracked so the UI can explain where state came from. Device path behavior Launchstation has user-facing paths such as mass: , usb: , hdd0:/partition/ and mc:/ , then resolves them into the concrete driver/mount needed for I/O. External-core launch code must distinguish that internal I/O path from the public path syntax the external core expects."},{"title":"LAUNCHELF.CNF","cat":"customize","url":"configuration.html#launchelf","text":"Important modern keys include: Key Purpose POPSTARTER_ELF Preferred POPStarter executable for VCD images. EMBER_DIR Ember ELF path; the historical key name is retained. Older folder values still resolve to their ember.elf. The executable can be staged into a game device's umbrella when needed. NEUTRINO_ELF Preferred Neutrino executable. REBOOT_IOP_ELFLOAD Generic normal-ELF reset behavior. Neutrino ISO handoff deliberately does not use it. CNF_Path Configuration load/save override. USBKBD_FILE / KBDMAP_FILE USB keyboard driver/map paths."},{"title":"Save locations","cat":"customize","url":"configuration.html#save-locations","text":"The dashboard can save classic configuration to the configured folder, next to the running ELF or a memory-card SYS-CONF target. The path used to load configuration is tracked so the UI can explain where state came from."},{"title":"Device path behavior","cat":"customize","url":"configuration.html#device-paths","text":"Launchstation has user-facing paths such as mass: , usb: , hdd0:/partition/ and mc:/ , then resolves them into the concrete driver/mount needed for I/O. External-core launch code must distinguish that internal I/O path from the public path syntax the external core expects."},{"title":"Display, Themes & BGM","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html","text":"Display, Themes & BGM The dashboard is PS2-inspired but deliberately configurable rather than forcing one presentation. Selection presentation Selection style can be changed instead of forcing a single hue/glow. Selected and unselected device cards use the same geometry; focus adds emphasis without resizing/reflowing the card. Icon backing Control/menu icon backing can be enabled or disabled explicitly. This option came from tester feedback rather than being baked into a single theme. Clock and date The dashboard clock can follow system 12/24-hour behavior or theme overrides, optionally show seconds and use configured date order/placement. File metadata retains seconds independently of the clock setting. Motion Top tabs, selection movement, OSD orbs and marquee effects use bounded smooth animation. The former fast sinusoidal header overshoot was removed because it could appear as top-of-dashboard video flicker on interlaced displays. Custom wallpaper: 4:3 aspect ratio Make your wallpaper on a 4:3 canvas . Use 640 × 480 PNG to preserve detail, or 320 × 240 for a smaller, lower-memory image like the starter example. Launchstation preserves aspect ratio, center-crops wider or taller inputs, and fills the screen. The decoder keeps wallpapers inside a 640 × 512 box, so a 4:3 source can retain 640 × 480 pixels. Smaller sources are not enlarged during decoding. A 640 × 480 texture uses about 1.17 MiB of decoded memory, four times the 320 × 240 option. This improves image detail at the existing output modes; it does not itself change the video output resolution. The 640 × 448 logical UI coordinate system is not the required artwork ratio. Example PNG: 320 × 240, exactly 4:3, with illustrative guides. You can use it as a test wallpaper or replace the drawing with your own artwork. Download example background.png · Matching theme.cfg · Complete example folder Format and composition tips Format: PNG, maximum file size 1.5 MB and source dimensions 2048 × 2048. A non-interlaced RGB or indexed 320 × 240 PNG is an easy choice. Safe area: keep important subject matter away from the extreme edges. CRT overscan and screen centering vary, and dashboard panels or hints may cover parts of the image. Contrast: use a dark or subdued background behind text and adjust theme brightness, panel opacity and text colours if needed. Other ratios: 320 × 256 is 5:4, and 320 × 224 is 10:7; both are supported inputs but are not the ideal 4:3 canvas. 16:9 sources are cropped horizontally. How to enable one For a single image, open Settings > Appearance > Customize theme > Background > Wallpaper image... , then choose a PNG and save your Settings changes. To load the example theme, place theme.cfg and background.png together inside a folder under THEMES/ next to the Launchstation ELF (or in the dashboard configuration folder). Choose Settings > Appearance > Theme files & resets > Load theme from folder... , select that folder and save. For a theme.cfg file, background = image and image = background.png select the static wallpaper. The separate wallpaper = focused option uses the artwork of the selected application and is not needed for a custom static image. Missing or invalid wallpaper files fall back to the theme gradient. Background music BGM.ADP ships in the current rolling ZIP/PSU packages but remains optional for standalone ELF installs. Put it next to the executable when installed manually. A missing music file does not prevent the dashboard from booting. Website cursor orbs The GitHub Pages site has a separate top-header cursor-orb toggle. Orbs are on by default on desktop and off by default on mobile/coarse-pointer devices. Your choice is saved locally, and switching off stops the animation loop. This does not change the PS2 dashboard theme. Fonts and text weight Settings > Appearance > Customize theme > Text > Font offers Roboto (default), System and Emotion Engine. Style selects Regular or Bold. Emotion Engine uses its original regular/bold outlines, with printable ASCII/Latin-1 characters and symbols, by Pixel Sagas. Roboto and System retain their existing extra stroke pass for Bold style. Small and medium font atlases are corrected for their stored alpha boost. The Text Editor also follows the selected font and Style on its fixed-width document grid. Text shadow has its own setting. Reset display/theme The reset action restores video mode/centering and project theme defaults, useful when experimentation makes text, selection or offsets difficult to recover manually."},{"title":"Selection presentation","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#selection","text":"Selection style can be changed instead of forcing a single hue/glow. Selected and unselected device cards use the same geometry; focus adds emphasis without resizing/reflowing the card."},{"title":"Icon backing","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#backing","text":"Control/menu icon backing can be enabled or disabled explicitly. This option came from tester feedback rather than being baked into a single theme."},{"title":"Clock and date","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#clock","text":"The dashboard clock can follow system 12/24-hour behavior or theme overrides, optionally show seconds and use configured date order/placement. File metadata retains seconds independently of the clock setting."},{"title":"Motion","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#motion","text":"Top tabs, selection movement, OSD orbs and marquee effects use bounded smooth animation. The former fast sinusoidal header overshoot was removed because it could appear as top-of-dashboard video flicker on interlaced displays."},{"title":"Custom wallpaper: 4:3 aspect ratio","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#wallpaper-size","text":"Make your wallpaper on a 4:3 canvas . Use 640 × 480 PNG to preserve detail, or 320 × 240 for a smaller, lower-memory image like the starter example. Launchstation preserves aspect ratio, center-crops wider or taller inputs, and fills the screen. The decoder keeps wallpapers inside a 640 × 512 box, so a 4:3 source can retain 640 × 480 pixels. Smaller sources are not enlarged during decoding. A 640 × 480 texture uses about 1.17 MiB of decoded memory, four times the 320 × 240 option. This improves image detail at the existing output modes; it does not itself change the video output resolution. The 640 × 448 logical UI coordinate system is not the required artwork ratio. Example PNG: 320 × 240, exactly 4:3, with illustrative guides. You can use it as a test wallpaper or replace the drawing with your own artwork. Download example background.png · Matching theme.cfg · Complete example folder"},{"title":"Format and composition tips","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#wallpaper-tips","text":"Format: PNG, maximum file size 1.5 MB and source dimensions 2048 × 2048. A non-interlaced RGB or indexed 320 × 240 PNG is an easy choice. Safe area: keep important subject matter away from the extreme edges. CRT overscan and screen centering vary, and dashboard panels or hints may cover parts of the image. Contrast: use a dark or subdued background behind text and adjust theme brightness, panel opacity and text colours if needed. Other ratios: 320 × 256 is 5:4, and 320 × 224 is 10:7; both are supported inputs but are not the ideal 4:3 canvas. 16:9 sources are cropped horizontally."},{"title":"How to enable one","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#wallpaper-setup","text":"For a single image, open Settings > Appearance > Customize theme > Background > Wallpaper image... , then choose a PNG and save your Settings changes. To load the example theme, place theme.cfg and background.png together inside a folder under THEMES/ next to the Launchstation ELF (or in the dashboard configuration folder). Choose Settings > Appearance > Theme files & resets > Load theme from folder... , select that folder and save. For a theme.cfg file, background = image and image = background.png select the static wallpaper. The separate wallpaper = focused option uses the artwork of the selected application and is not needed for a custom static image. Missing or invalid wallpaper files fall back to the theme gradient."},{"title":"Background music","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#bgm","text":"BGM.ADP ships in the current rolling ZIP/PSU packages but remains optional for standalone ELF installs. Put it next to the executable when installed manually. A missing music file does not prevent the dashboard from booting."},{"title":"Website cursor orbs","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#website-orbs","text":"The GitHub Pages site has a separate top-header cursor-orb toggle. Orbs are on by default on desktop and off by default on mobile/coarse-pointer devices. Your choice is saved locally, and switching off stops the animation loop. This does not change the PS2 dashboard theme."},{"title":"Fonts and text weight","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#text-weight","text":"Settings > Appearance > Customize theme > Text > Font offers Roboto (default), System and Emotion Engine. Style selects Regular or Bold. Emotion Engine uses its original regular/bold outlines, with printable ASCII/Latin-1 characters and symbols, by Pixel Sagas. Roboto and System retain their existing extra stroke pass for Bold style. Small and medium font atlases are corrected for their stored alpha boost. The Text Editor also follows the selected font and Style on its fixed-width document grid. Text shadow has its own setting."},{"title":"Reset display/theme","cat":"customize","url":"themes.html#reset","text":"The reset action restores video mode/centering and project theme defaults, useful when experimentation makes text, selection or offsets difficult to recover manually."},{"title":"Wiki & Guide Index","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html","text":"LAUNCHSTATION Wiki & Guide Index This version-controlled guide index is generated with the site so navigation, hardware safety, documentation and the release packages can be checked together. Start and navigation Install and first boot Current four-tab dashboard and Applications library File Browser and file operations Settings, startup and loader paths Storage, launchers and hardware Device support and USB unit diagnostics HDD/APA header tools MMCE devices Neutrino PS2 ISO handoff POPStarter PS1 VCD handoff Ember PS1 folder layout and USB handoff Networking and UDPFS Backups and releases Rolling ZIP/PSU downloads and native updater Troubleshooting and hardware test reports Markdown wiki source The editable Markdown guide collection is kept in the main repository at docs/wiki/ . A separate synchronization workflow publishes these Markdown guides and the site reference pages into the standalone GitHub Wiki . It preserves unmanaged pages and wiki history. The Pages workflow publishes the site independently; both use the reviewed documentation sources. Build SDK Launchstation uses TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK for local and CI builds. See developer setup and the Wiki build guide . Attribution and maintenance Read Credits & Licenses and the third-party component inventory . Update the developer notes when the actual UI, safety contract or release workflow changes."},{"title":"Start and navigation","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#start","text":"Install and first boot Current four-tab dashboard and Applications library File Browser and file operations Settings, startup and loader paths"},{"title":"Storage, launchers and hardware","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#devices","text":"Device support and USB unit diagnostics HDD/APA header tools MMCE devices Neutrino PS2 ISO handoff POPStarter PS1 VCD handoff Ember PS1 folder layout and USB handoff Networking and UDPFS"},{"title":"Backups and releases","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#recovery","text":"Rolling ZIP/PSU downloads and native updater Troubleshooting and hardware test reports"},{"title":"Markdown wiki source","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#wiki-source","text":"The editable Markdown guide collection is kept in the main repository at docs/wiki/ . A separate synchronization workflow publishes these Markdown guides and the site reference pages into the standalone GitHub Wiki . It preserves unmanaged pages and wiki history. The Pages workflow publishes the site independently; both use the reviewed documentation sources."},{"title":"Build SDK","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#build-sdk","text":"Launchstation uses TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK for local and CI builds. See developer setup and the Wiki build guide ."},{"title":"Attribution and maintenance","cat":"reference","url":"wiki.html#contributions","text":"Read Credits & Licenses and the third-party component inventory . Update the developer notes when the actual UI, safety contract or release workflow changes."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html","text":"Troubleshooting Identify the failing layer first: hardware/device, filesystem, Launchstation launch preparation, external core, or the target application/game. Device says Not checked Open it. Not checked is intentionally actionable: the lazy driver has not yet established presence. A confirmed Not connected result is different. USB path/unit changed Do not assume mass0: . Multiple drives, MX4SIO and attach order can affect unit numbering. Start from the dashboard's USB / MASS device and let the root discovery logic identify what is available. MC-to-MMCE paste fails before writing Checking destination (-1) with zero bytes copied can come from older MMCE driver code treating a missing destination file as a generic failure. The corrected driver translates the firmware's completed missing-path response to the filesystem error expected by the copy engine. Communication failures remain errors, and existing-file replacements still use staging and verification. This correction is embedded in the rebuilt ELF; replacing a sidecar file does not update the driver. PS2 ISO will not start Browse/read the ISO from the same source device. Confirm it is an ISO9660 image. Set a Neutrino path explicitly to remove search-order ambiguity. Confirm Neutrino has modern config/system.toml or legacy adjacent system.toml . Try a known-good ISO on the same backend. PS1 VCD will not start Confirm the VCD is in a supported POPStarter layout, then set POPStarter explicitly. HDD POPS layouts have special argument construction; a raw browser path is not necessarily a valid POPStarter command line. Colored bars before opening ELFs Launchstation can intentionally draw a short Game ID identification signal for compatible video adapters. To disable the colored bars, open Settings > Launching > Game ID output and turn off the application Game ID launch signal. Normal start-up boot-check text is hidden in production builds. Top-of-dashboard flicker The known fast selector oscillation has been removed. If flicker persists, report video mode, display/cable, interlace/progressive mode, theme, and whether the symptom occurs in the classic UI too. Update check: HTTPS MAC error HTTPS read: SSL-Verification of the message MAC failed (-29056) means TLS could not authenticate the received data. The standard PS2BUILD target includes corrections for an undersized socket receive-metadata buffer and stale head/tail copies after nonblocking reads. If an older updater fails this way, install the rebuilt ELF manually before testing updates again. Certificate-chain and console-date errors are separate checks; certificate verification remains required. Good bug report Exact commit/build and package or packed ELF SHA-256. Console model and video mode. Source device/backend and filesystem. Minimal reproduction steps. Whether classic browser and dashboard differ. For Neutrino/POPStarter, external-core version and resolved path."},{"title":"Device says Not checked","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#not-checked","text":"Open it. Not checked is intentionally actionable: the lazy driver has not yet established presence. A confirmed Not connected result is different."},{"title":"USB path/unit changed","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#usb","text":"Do not assume mass0: . Multiple drives, MX4SIO and attach order can affect unit numbering. Start from the dashboard's USB / MASS device and let the root discovery logic identify what is available."},{"title":"MC-to-MMCE paste fails before writing","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#mmce-copy","text":"Checking destination (-1) with zero bytes copied can come from older MMCE driver code treating a missing destination file as a generic failure. The corrected driver translates the firmware's completed missing-path response to the filesystem error expected by the copy engine. Communication failures remain errors, and existing-file replacements still use staging and verification. This correction is embedded in the rebuilt ELF; replacing a sidecar file does not update the driver."},{"title":"PS2 ISO will not start","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#iso","text":"Browse/read the ISO from the same source device. Confirm it is an ISO9660 image. Set a Neutrino path explicitly to remove search-order ambiguity. Confirm Neutrino has modern config/system.toml or legacy adjacent system.toml . Try a known-good ISO on the same backend."},{"title":"PS1 VCD will not start","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#vcd","text":"Confirm the VCD is in a supported POPStarter layout, then set POPStarter explicitly. HDD POPS layouts have special argument construction; a raw browser path is not necessarily a valid POPStarter command line."},{"title":"Colored bars before opening ELFs","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#launch-colors","text":"Launchstation can intentionally draw a short Game ID identification signal for compatible video adapters. To disable the colored bars, open Settings > Launching > Game ID output and turn off the application Game ID launch signal. Normal start-up boot-check text is hidden in production builds."},{"title":"Top-of-dashboard flicker","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#flicker","text":"The known fast selector oscillation has been removed. If flicker persists, report video mode, display/cable, interlace/progressive mode, theme, and whether the symptom occurs in the classic UI too."},{"title":"Update check: HTTPS MAC error","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#update-tls","text":"HTTPS read: SSL-Verification of the message MAC failed (-29056) means TLS could not authenticate the received data. The standard PS2BUILD target includes corrections for an undersized socket receive-metadata buffer and stale head/tail copies after nonblocking reads. If an older updater fails this way, install the rebuilt ELF manually before testing updates again. Certificate-chain and console-date errors are separate checks; certificate verification remains required."},{"title":"Good bug report","cat":"reference","url":"troubleshooting.html#report","text":"Exact commit/build and package or packed ELF SHA-256. Console model and video mode. Source device/backend and filesystem. Minimal reproduction steps. Whether classic browser and dashboard differ. For Neutrino/POPStarter, external-core version and resolved path."},{"title":"FAQ","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html","text":"Frequently Asked Questions Where did the separate Apps and Devices tabs go? There are now four top-level tabs: Home, Files, Tools, Settings. The full Applications library is on Home; Files opens the live native File Browser. Devices are reached from the browser root. Current dashboard navigation . Why is an ELF absent from Applications? The library scans only configured folders. Use Settings > Home & applications > Application library > Application folders... , then Refresh library now . The current default scan roots include mc:/APPS , mc:/BOOT , mass:/APPS and mass:/BOOT , one subdirectory deep; launchable files on other roots may still work directly through Files. What does SELECT do now? While in Home > Applications, SELECT cycles Grid, List and Carousel views. The visible top-level tabs are Home, Files, Tools and Settings. See Controls for the current mapping. Are Neutrino, POPStarter or Ember included? No. All three are external projects. A PS2 .ISO is handed to Neutrino , a PS1 .VCD to POPStarter , and a PS1 .CUE / .BIN / .EXE in an Ember game folder to Ember . The image format determines the launcher; there is no preferred-core switch. Must Ember be at /EMBER/ on the device root? No. The umbrella folder can have any name and location when the supported <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ layout is retained. Its bios.bin is supplied by the user, and ember.elf is either present or staged from the configured Ember ELF path. USB launches rebuild the storage IOP stack before the final keep-IOP handoff. Why can two USB devices behave differently? Enumeration order and logical mount numbers can change. The loader does not assume mass0: is always the same drive. Tools includes an USB unit diagnostics action; an explicit probe can still block inside a synchronous IOP RPC. Do not unplug active media during writes. How are rolling releases published? The latest packages are published after the verified master-build workflow. The native updater requires matching verified app packages and preserves existing sidecars. Compile/CI success does not guarantee real-PS2 hardware acceptance. Is there an offline documentation/wiki copy? Yes. The site generator produces HTML, styles, scripts, search index and a ZIP archive. Read the wiki/guide index or run python docs-site/build.py --out build/docs --zip build/Launchstation-Documentation.zip . Where are third-party credits? See Credits & Licenses and the dependency and provenance inventory ."},{"title":"Where did the separate Apps and Devices tabs go?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#tabs","text":"There are now four top-level tabs: Home, Files, Tools, Settings. The full Applications library is on Home; Files opens the live native File Browser. Devices are reached from the browser root. Current dashboard navigation ."},{"title":"Why is an ELF absent from Applications?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#scan","text":"The library scans only configured folders. Use Settings > Home & applications > Application library > Application folders... , then Refresh library now . The current default scan roots include mc:/APPS , mc:/BOOT , mass:/APPS and mass:/BOOT , one subdirectory deep; launchable files on other roots may still work directly through Files."},{"title":"What does SELECT do now?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#select-control","text":"While in Home > Applications, SELECT cycles Grid, List and Carousel views. The visible top-level tabs are Home, Files, Tools and Settings. See Controls for the current mapping."},{"title":"Are Neutrino, POPStarter or Ember included?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#cores","text":"No. All three are external projects. A PS2 .ISO is handed to Neutrino , a PS1 .VCD to POPStarter , and a PS1 .CUE / .BIN / .EXE in an Ember game folder to Ember . The image format determines the launcher; there is no preferred-core switch."},{"title":"Must Ember be at /EMBER/ on the device root?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#ember","text":"No. The umbrella folder can have any name and location when the supported <umbrella>/games/<Game>/ layout is retained. Its bios.bin is supplied by the user, and ember.elf is either present or staged from the configured Ember ELF path. USB launches rebuild the storage IOP stack before the final keep-IOP handoff."},{"title":"Why can two USB devices behave differently?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#usb","text":"Enumeration order and logical mount numbers can change. The loader does not assume mass0: is always the same drive. Tools includes an USB unit diagnostics action; an explicit probe can still block inside a synchronous IOP RPC. Do not unplug active media during writes."},{"title":"How are rolling releases published?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#update","text":"The latest packages are published after the verified master-build workflow. The native updater requires matching verified app packages and preserves existing sidecars. Compile/CI success does not guarantee real-PS2 hardware acceptance."},{"title":"Is there an offline documentation/wiki copy?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#docs","text":"Yes. The site generator produces HTML, styles, scripts, search index and a ZIP archive. Read the wiki/guide index or run python docs-site/build.py --out build/docs --zip build/Launchstation-Documentation.zip ."},{"title":"Where are third-party credits?","cat":"reference","url":"faq.html#licenses","text":"See Credits & Licenses and the dependency and provenance inventory ."},{"title":"Developer Notes","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html","text":"Developer and Documentation Maintenance Keep source behavior, user instructions, CI contracts, release packaging and third-party attribution synchronized. Source of truth The public site is generated from docs-site/content/*.html plus docs-site/manifest.json by docs-site/build.py . docs/wiki/*.md contains the version-controlled, wiki-style guide set. A separate synchronization workflow publishes these guides and the site reference pages to GitHub Wiki, preserving unmanaged pages and history. Do not hand-edit the generated gh-pages branch: the publishing workflow replaces it. TechWritesCode PS2BUILD SDK Launchstation is built and validated with the PS2BUILD SDK by TechWritesCode . It supplies our EE/IOP toolchains, SDK packages and ps2.yaml build workflow for local development and CI. Follow the official guide to install the SDK, then run the commands below. python3 scripts/gen_githash.py ps2build build python scripts/configure_ds34.py ps2build build -c .ps2-ds34.yaml -o build-ds34 Regenerate githash.h after changing commits. It supplies the installed commit ID for Build information and the updater. Source archives without Git metadata identify themselves as local and cannot verify update ancestry. CI requires a checkout and verifies the full commit ID in both compiled release ELFs before packaging. Build and validate the documentation python3 docs-site/build.py --out site --zip Launchstation-Documentation.zip The generator checks the page inventory, unresolved template fragments, site assets, and internal HTML links before writing the offline archive. Main CI gates Compiled host regression contracts for device/USB/PAD timing, navigation, settings, MMCE filesystem and Game ID switching, and update safeguards. Standard and DS34 cross-builds and relevant embedded loader/IOP checks. Native HTTPS dependency cross-build and release package validation. Generated documentation and offline ZIP validation. Hardware-only timing, USB bridge behavior, console model quirks and external game-core interaction remain separate from automated acceptance. Rolling release and Pages Verified pushes/merges to master can update the rolling latest release , including matching standard/DS34 ZIP and PSU packages. The documentation workflow builds from the same authored source and publishes gh-pages automatically. A docs-only commit can therefore produce a new rolling build without implying runtime behavior changed. Native updater The updater validates the release's ancestry and the expected package digest, selects the correct controller variant, stages a verified PSU package and ELF, and retains a rollback path. It can restore absent updater CA and BGM sidecars without overwriting custom files. See Downloads . Runtime architecture Neutrino uses an already mounted backing-store stack for quick-boot, while Ember's USB path first performs a controlled clean IOP storage-stack rebuild and remounts the same game identity before its final keep-IOP launch. Neither follows the ordinary ELF reset preference. Local MMCE history is verified before optional Drive upload. Attribution discipline Every external project mentioned in READMEs, site pages and guides must appear in Credits with its role identified. Update the attribution inventory when vendoring modules, incorporating sources or adding external handoff targets. Preserve each original file-level license; a reference is not the same as a redistributed component."},{"title":"Source of truth","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#repo","text":"The public site is generated from docs-site/content/*.html plus docs-site/manifest.json by docs-site/build.py . docs/wiki/*.md contains the version-controlled, wiki-style guide set. A separate synchronization workflow publishes these guides and the site reference pages to GitHub Wiki, preserving unmanaged pages and history. Do not hand-edit the generated gh-pages branch: the publishing workflow replaces it."},{"title":"TechWritesCode PS2BUILD SDK","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#sdk","text":"Launchstation is built and validated with the PS2BUILD SDK by TechWritesCode . It supplies our EE/IOP toolchains, SDK packages and ps2.yaml build workflow for local development and CI. Follow the official guide to install the SDK, then run the commands below. python3 scripts/gen_githash.py ps2build build python scripts/configure_ds34.py ps2build build -c .ps2-ds34.yaml -o build-ds34 Regenerate githash.h after changing commits. It supplies the installed commit ID for Build information and the updater. Source archives without Git metadata identify themselves as local and cannot verify update ancestry. CI requires a checkout and verifies the full commit ID in both compiled release ELFs before packaging."},{"title":"Build and validate the documentation","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#build","text":"python3 docs-site/build.py --out site --zip Launchstation-Documentation.zip The generator checks the page inventory, unresolved template fragments, site assets, and internal HTML links before writing the offline archive."},{"title":"Main CI gates","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#ci","text":"Compiled host regression contracts for device/USB/PAD timing, navigation, settings, MMCE filesystem and Game ID switching, and update safeguards. Standard and DS34 cross-builds and relevant embedded loader/IOP checks. Native HTTPS dependency cross-build and release package validation. Generated documentation and offline ZIP validation. Hardware-only timing, USB bridge behavior, console model quirks and external game-core interaction remain separate from automated acceptance."},{"title":"Rolling release and Pages","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#release","text":"Verified pushes/merges to master can update the rolling latest release , including matching standard/DS34 ZIP and PSU packages. The documentation workflow builds from the same authored source and publishes gh-pages automatically. A docs-only commit can therefore produce a new rolling build without implying runtime behavior changed."},{"title":"Native updater","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#native-update","text":"The updater validates the release's ancestry and the expected package digest, selects the correct controller variant, stages a verified PSU package and ELF, and retains a rollback path. It can restore absent updater CA and BGM sidecars without overwriting custom files. See Downloads ."},{"title":"Runtime architecture","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#architecture","text":"Neutrino uses an already mounted backing-store stack for quick-boot, while Ember's USB path first performs a controlled clean IOP storage-stack rebuild and remounts the same game identity before its final keep-IOP launch. Neither follows the ordinary ELF reset preference. Local MMCE history is verified before optional Drive upload."},{"title":"Attribution discipline","cat":"reference","url":"developers.html#credits","text":"Every external project mentioned in READMEs, site pages and guides must appear in Credits with its role identified. Update the attribution inventory when vendoring modules, incorporating sources or adding external handoff targets. Preserve each original file-level license; a reference is not the same as a redistributed component."},{"title":"Credits & Licenses","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html","text":"Credits, Third-Party Projects & Licenses LAUNCHSTATION builds on decades of PS2 homebrew. This page distinguishes upstream code, checked-in components, external programs launched by the user, referenced projects and community contributions. Project lineage and maintainers wLaunchELF_R3Z by R3Z3N / Saildot4K is the direct upstream base. The earlier wLaunchELF / uLaunchELF / LaunchELF developers and ps2dev community retain credit for the underlying launcher lineage. johnson-cooper created this fork and transferred maintenance; NathanNeurotic (Ripto) is its current maintainer. Special thanks to techwritescode for collaboration, TnA for insight and support, oMrRexD for PC/R3Z ecosystem collaboration and reference, and zackcage6 and FitthFox for dashboard and File Browser testing and UX feedback. Contributors and testers also appear in the repository's Git history, merged pull requests and release notes. Neutrino MMCE Game ID selection follows wLaunchELF_R3Z 4.80's adapter and VMC slot handling , pinned to commit 39fb5bc . Our build SDK Launchstation is built and validated with the PS2BUILD SDK by TechWritesCode . It supplies our EE/IOP toolchains, SDK packages and ps2.yaml build workflow for local development and CI. Follow the official guide to install the SDK, then run the commands below. Upstream PS2SDK and community links below preserve source ancestry and component licenses. PC CARD CompactFlash foundation Credit to mihhru and his PS2CF project for the early-PS2 CF PIO/ATA driver foundation and host tests. The isolated source port in iop/pccf/ preserves component notices and the PS2SDK-derived hardware license. Native CF browsing and console compatibility remain under development; this credit does not claim verified support. External projects and dependency inventory Attribution below does not mean every third-party program is distributed by LAUNCHSTATION. The role of each project is specified. wLaunchELF_R3Z — Direct upstream base by R3Z3N / Saildot4K; earlier LaunchELF, uLaunchELF and wLaunchELF contributors retain credit. ps2dev / PS2SDK — Upstream foundations and original authorship of vendored memory-card, HDD, networking and filesystem code; our builds use TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK. ps2-mmce/mmceman — MMCE driver lineage and device protocol: local driver sources and precompiled module. Credit the upstream MMCE developers, including Wisi and Maximus32 for earlier SIO2 work. RiptOPL / Open-PS2-Loader — Source/reference of the updated usbd-ra USB driver imported during USB recovery. PS2IP socket RPC server — ps2dev contributors; vendored under iop/ps2ips_rpc/ with its Academic Free License 2.0 and pinned-source notice. Built as a separate IOP target for clock sync and Internet clients. ps2ftpd — Jesper Svennevid's FTP server; checked-in legacy source at iop/oldlibs/ps2ftpd/, accompanied by Academic Free License v2.0. libcdvd (legacy) — Legacy checked-in IOP CDVD library; copyright A. Lee and Nicholas Van Veen. Its file-level license restricts commercial use. See iop/oldlibs/libcdvd/license.txt. DS34 controller drivers — Inherited PS3/PS4 controller support and local DS34 driver sources, with credit to Alex Parrado and the R3Z/PS2 community. audsrv — Locally retained PS2 audio server package (packages/world/audsrv); LGPL-2.0 identified in packages/README.md. libogg / libvorbis — Retained compiled codec packages in packages/world/ogg and packages/world/vorbis; BSD-style licensing described in packages/README.md. Mbed TLS — HTTPS dependency for the built-in updater, built from upstream source under Apache-2.0. Roboto font — Bundled font notice: docs/licenses/Roboto-COPYRIGHT.txt. Emotion Engine Regular/Bold — Neale Davidson / Pixel Sagas. Embedded font atlases; original license: docs/licenses/Emotion-Engine-LICENSE.txt. Source versions and regeneration: docs/font-generation.md. Material Design Icons rocket-launch — Pictogrammers / MDI contributors. Vendored SVG adapted for the shared icon atlas; Apache-2.0 notice: docs/licenses/Material-Design-Icons-LICENSE.txt. Neutrino — External PS2 ISO loader maintained independently; the ELF and its configuration are not bundled. POPStarter — krHACKen's independent PS1 VCD launcher, not included with Launchstation. Ember — Gageformer's external PS1 core, not bundled; beta builds have their own distribution terms. PS2Link / PS2Client — Upstream network development and remote-access ecosystem (including the separate PC client). UDPFSD server — pcm720's PC-side UDPFS server referenced by supported network storage workflows. OSDMenu / launcher — pcm720's reference for KELF forwarders and PS2 boot workflows; referenced, not necessarily included. KELFTool — xfwcfw's separate KELF tooling referenced for PS2KEYS.dat and KELF workflows. HDD-OSD Icon Generator — CosmicScale's helper for HDD/APA icon resources; referenced, not bundled. PixelFX RetroGem — External hardware ecosystem for the optional RetroGem Game ID feature. oMrRexD / R3Z PC ecosystem — Compatibility and integration reference for PC-side remote commands and transfers. Additional graphics, image and bootloader projects gsKit and dmaKit — the PS2 dashboard graphics libraries; gsKit credits Chris \"Neovanglist\" Gilbert and contributors, under upstream Academic Free License version 2.0. libpng and Independent JPEG Group / libjpeg — image decoding and library interfaces behind the native viewer. See relevant linked library notices. PS2BBL by El_isra and PS2BBLE by R3Z3N — independent PS2 bootloader projects, not synonymous with Launchstation's exploit installer. LoadBOOTer — a named option in the inherited memory-card exploit installer; authoritative author and source are not verified in this repository audit. ESR, FreeMcBoot and PSBBN — separate boot tools, software environments or compatible formats referenced in the docs; their code is not implied to be distributed. Further library and subsystem credits The native image viewer relies on libpng and libjpeg support; the EE/IOP graphics, filesystems, audio and networking stacks include PS2SDK and upstream library contributors. Check the originating source file headers, included package metadata and the relevant upstream license notices when redistributing builds or source archives. The PS2Key material, user games, PS1 BIOS and external emulator executables are not supplied by this repository. License notices and distribution There is no repository-root LICENSE file at this audit snapshot. Do not infer one blanket license for every file or third-party module. Checked-in notices include ps2ftpd AFL-2.0 , libcdvd's restrictive historical terms , and Roboto's notice . See the detailed attribution and license inventory for provenance and unresolved checks. Keep original notices intact. Wiki and guides · Developer notes · Project repository"},{"title":"Project lineage and maintainers","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#lineage","text":"wLaunchELF_R3Z by R3Z3N / Saildot4K is the direct upstream base. The earlier wLaunchELF / uLaunchELF / LaunchELF developers and ps2dev community retain credit for the underlying launcher lineage. johnson-cooper created this fork and transferred maintenance; NathanNeurotic (Ripto) is its current maintainer. Special thanks to techwritescode for collaboration, TnA for insight and support, oMrRexD for PC/R3Z ecosystem collaboration and reference, and zackcage6 and FitthFox for dashboard and File Browser testing and UX feedback. Contributors and testers also appear in the repository's Git history, merged pull requests and release notes. Neutrino MMCE Game ID selection follows wLaunchELF_R3Z 4.80's adapter and VMC slot handling , pinned to commit 39fb5bc ."},{"title":"Our build SDK","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#sdk","text":"Launchstation is built and validated with the PS2BUILD SDK by TechWritesCode . It supplies our EE/IOP toolchains, SDK packages and ps2.yaml build workflow for local development and CI. Follow the official guide to install the SDK, then run the commands below. Upstream PS2SDK and community links below preserve source ancestry and component licenses."},{"title":"PC CARD CompactFlash foundation","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#pc-card-cf","text":"Credit to mihhru and his PS2CF project for the early-PS2 CF PIO/ATA driver foundation and host tests. The isolated source port in iop/pccf/ preserves component notices and the PS2SDK-derived hardware license. Native CF browsing and console compatibility remain under development; this credit does not claim verified support."},{"title":"External projects and dependency inventory","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#projects","text":"Attribution below does not mean every third-party program is distributed by LAUNCHSTATION. The role of each project is specified. wLaunchELF_R3Z — Direct upstream base by R3Z3N / Saildot4K; earlier LaunchELF, uLaunchELF and wLaunchELF contributors retain credit. ps2dev / PS2SDK — Upstream foundations and original authorship of vendored memory-card, HDD, networking and filesystem code; our builds use TechWritesCode's PS2BUILD SDK. ps2-mmce/mmceman — MMCE driver lineage and device protocol: local driver sources and precompiled module. Credit the upstream MMCE developers, including Wisi and Maximus32 for earlier SIO2 work. RiptOPL / Open-PS2-Loader — Source/reference of the updated usbd-ra USB driver imported during USB recovery. PS2IP socket RPC server — ps2dev contributors; vendored under iop/ps2ips_rpc/ with its Academic Free License 2.0 and pinned-source notice. Built as a separate IOP target for clock sync and Internet clients. ps2ftpd — Jesper Svennevid's FTP server; checked-in legacy source at iop/oldlibs/ps2ftpd/, accompanied by Academic Free License v2.0. libcdvd (legacy) — Legacy checked-in IOP CDVD library; copyright A. Lee and Nicholas Van Veen. Its file-level license restricts commercial use. See iop/oldlibs/libcdvd/license.txt. DS34 controller drivers — Inherited PS3/PS4 controller support and local DS34 driver sources, with credit to Alex Parrado and the R3Z/PS2 community. audsrv — Locally retained PS2 audio server package (packages/world/audsrv); LGPL-2.0 identified in packages/README.md. libogg / libvorbis — Retained compiled codec packages in packages/world/ogg and packages/world/vorbis; BSD-style licensing described in packages/README.md. Mbed TLS — HTTPS dependency for the built-in updater, built from upstream source under Apache-2.0. Roboto font — Bundled font notice: docs/licenses/Roboto-COPYRIGHT.txt. Emotion Engine Regular/Bold — Neale Davidson / Pixel Sagas. Embedded font atlases; original license: docs/licenses/Emotion-Engine-LICENSE.txt. Source versions and regeneration: docs/font-generation.md. Material Design Icons rocket-launch — Pictogrammers / MDI contributors. Vendored SVG adapted for the shared icon atlas; Apache-2.0 notice: docs/licenses/Material-Design-Icons-LICENSE.txt. Neutrino — External PS2 ISO loader maintained independently; the ELF and its configuration are not bundled. POPStarter — krHACKen's independent PS1 VCD launc"},{"title":"Additional graphics, image and bootloader projects","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#additional-credits","text":"gsKit and dmaKit — the PS2 dashboard graphics libraries; gsKit credits Chris \"Neovanglist\" Gilbert and contributors, under upstream Academic Free License version 2.0. libpng and Independent JPEG Group / libjpeg — image decoding and library interfaces behind the native viewer. See relevant linked library notices. PS2BBL by El_isra and PS2BBLE by R3Z3N — independent PS2 bootloader projects, not synonymous with Launchstation's exploit installer. LoadBOOTer — a named option in the inherited memory-card exploit installer; authoritative author and source are not verified in this repository audit. ESR, FreeMcBoot and PSBBN — separate boot tools, software environments or compatible formats referenced in the docs; their code is not implied to be distributed."},{"title":"Further library and subsystem credits","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#other","text":"The native image viewer relies on libpng and libjpeg support; the EE/IOP graphics, filesystems, audio and networking stacks include PS2SDK and upstream library contributors. Check the originating source file headers, included package metadata and the relevant upstream license notices when redistributing builds or source archives. The PS2Key material, user games, PS1 BIOS and external emulator executables are not supplied by this repository."},{"title":"License notices and distribution","cat":"reference","url":"credits.html#licenses","text":"There is no repository-root LICENSE file at this audit snapshot. Do not infer one blanket license for every file or third-party module. Checked-in notices include ps2ftpd AFL-2.0 , libcdvd's restrictive historical terms , and Roboto's notice . See the detailed attribution and license inventory for provenance and unresolved checks. Keep original notices intact. Wiki and guides · Developer notes · Project repository"},{"title":"Privacy Policy","cat":"reference","url":"privacy.html","text":"Privacy Policy Launchstation is free, open-source PlayStation 2 homebrew. It has no accounts, no analytics and no servers of its own. This page explains the little data it handles and where it goes. Summary Launchstation does not collect, sell or share personal data. There is no telemetry, advertising or tracking. Everything Launchstation stores stays on your own PS2 storage (memory card, USB, HDD and similar). Network features run only when you use them, and talk directly to the services listed below. Other network features Check for updates contacts the GitHub API and downloads release files from GitHub when you ask it to. GitHub's own privacy statement applies to those requests. Clock synchronization , when used, asks 0.pool.ntp.org for the current time. No personal data is sent. Network file sharing (host/UDPFS and similar) connects only to computers you configure on your own network. Like any internet request, these services can see your IP address. Launchstation adds no identifiers of its own. Children Launchstation does not knowingly collect data from anyone, including children. Changes and contact Changes to this policy are published on this page and in the project's GitHub repository . Questions can be raised as a GitHub issue. Last updated: 24 October 2026."},{"title":"Summary","cat":"reference","url":"privacy.html#summary","text":"Launchstation does not collect, sell or share personal data. There is no telemetry, advertising or tracking. Everything Launchstation stores stays on your own PS2 storage (memory card, USB, HDD and similar). Network features run only when you use them, and talk directly to the services listed below."},{"title":"Other network features","cat":"reference","url":"privacy.html#other-network","text":"Check for updates contacts the GitHub API and downloads release files from GitHub when you ask it to. GitHub's own privacy statement applies to those requests. Clock synchronization , when used, asks 0.pool.ntp.org for the current time. No personal data is sent. Network file sharing (host/UDPFS and similar) connects only to computers you configure on your own network. Like any internet request, these services can see your IP address. Launchstation adds no identifiers of its own."},{"title":"Children","cat":"reference","url":"privacy.html#children","text":"Launchstation does not knowingly collect data from anyone, including children."},{"title":"Changes and contact","cat":"reference","url":"privacy.html#changes","text":"Changes to this policy are published on this page and in the project's GitHub repository . Questions can be raised as a GitHub issue. Last updated: 24 October 2026."}];
